{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLxpj6ut4TKqemZ4bqoU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Cu7EHTehF8djaXwhDsvMKA","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat .scratch/ts-rewrite/spec/11-snapshot.md; cat src/snapshot/snapshot.ts src/cli/runs.ts"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3724,"cache_read_input_tokens":52120,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3724},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0de572ae-0eae-4979-b0e7-cefd41e0bbfc","uuid":"4b4b2b99-1a7c-4c9e-aafc-1911dee62acc","timestamp":"2026-11-24T02:27:41.872Z","request_id":"req_011ChLxpiwF6uE9Jv2PWB1Mx","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Cu7EHTehF8djaXwhDsvMKA":{"command":"cd /Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-24-runs-checkout-and-prune && cat .scratch/ts-rewrite/spec/11-snapshot.md; cat src/snapshot/snapshot.ts src/cli/runs.ts"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01Cu7EHTehF8djaXwhDsvMKA":{"cwd":"/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-24-runs-checkout-and-prune"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Cu7EHTehF8djaXwhDsvMKA","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (45.4KB). Full output saved to: /Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-24-runs-checkout-and-prune/0de572ae-0eae-4979-b0e7-cefd41e0bbfc/tool-results/b2gczd9rb.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n# Area 11: Review snapshots, `runs checkout`, `runs prune`\n\n## Purpose\n\nA review snapshot pins exactly what one review read, as ordinary Git\nobjects in the reviewed repository's own object store, so a run record can\nlater be replayed or inspected against the same bytes. It captures the\nindex (what was staged) and the worktree (tracked files plus untracked,\nnon-ignored files, including uncommitted edits and deletions) as two tree\nobjects, joins them in one composite tree, anchors that tree with a commit,\nand points a private ref at the commit:\n\n```\nrefs/roast/snapshots/<run-id>  ->  commit \"roast run <run-id>\"\n                                     parents: HEAD [, base]\n                                     tree:\n                                       index/     (index tree)\n                                       worktree/  (worktree tree)\n```\n\nThe live index and the user's working tree are never written. Three things\nare deliberately not captured: `.gitignore`d files (`git add -A` skips them,\nmatching Roast's own scope rules), empty directories (Git cannot represent\nthem), and untracked, non-ignored files whose names match the secret\ndenylist (recorded in the run record as `excluded_paths`).\n\nThe same package provides the \"pin\": the worktree half alone, written as an\nunreferenced tree object before inference starts, so every reader in a\nreview (grounding, `read_file`, verifier context) sees one immutable tree\neven when no replayable snapshot can be recorded. The secret denylist is\nalso owned here and consumed by the scope and context areas.\n\nTwo `runs` subcommands operate on snapshots:\n\n- `npx roast runs checkout <run-id> [dir]` materializes a run's snapshot\n  into a detached linked worktree whose `git status`, `git diff HEAD`, and\n  `git diff --cached HEAD` reproduce the original state.\n- `npx roast runs prune` deletes aged refs under `refs/roast/snapshots/`.\n\nRun record layout and `meta.json` as a whole belong to the records area;\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0de572ae-0eae-4979-b0e7-cefd41e0bbfc","uuid":"695d93ec-a244-4094-962d-210996c2313e","timestamp":"2026-11-24T02:27:41.958Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Area 11: Review snapshots, `runs checkout`, `runs prune`\n\n## Purpose\n\nA review snapshot pins exactly what one review read, as ordinary Git\nobjects in the reviewed repository's own object store, so a run record can\nlater be replayed or inspected against the same bytes. It captures the\nindex (what was staged) and the worktree (tracked files plus untracked,\nnon-ignored files, including uncommitted edits and deletions) as two tree\nobjects, joins them in one composite tree, anchors that tree with a commit,\nand points a private ref at the commit:\n\n```\nrefs/roast/snapshots/<run-id>  ->  commit \"roast run <run-id>\"\n                                     parents: HEAD [, base]\n                                     tree:\n                                       index/     (index tree)\n                                       worktree/  (worktree tree)\n```\n\nThe live index and the user's working tree are never written. Three things\nare deliberately not captured: `.gitignore`d files (`git add -A` skips them,\nmatching Roast's own scope rules), empty directories (Git cannot represent\nthem), and untracked, non-ignored files whose names match the secret\ndenylist (recorded in the run record as `excluded_paths`).\n\nThe same package provides the \"pin\": the worktree half alone, written as an\nunreferenced tree object before inference starts, so every reader in a\nreview (grounding, `read_file`, verifier context) sees one immutable tree\neven when no replayable snapshot can be recorded. The secret denylist is\nalso owned here and consumed by the scope and context areas.\n\nTwo `runs` subcommands operate on snapshots:\n\n- `npx roast runs checkout <run-id> [dir]` materializes a run's snapshot\n  into a detached linked worktree whose `git status`, `git diff HEAD`, and\n  `git diff --cached HEAD` reproduce the original state.\n- `npx roast runs prune` deletes aged refs under `refs/roast/snapshots/`.\n\nRun record layout and `meta.json` as a whole belong to the records area;\nthis file specifies only the `snapshot` block and how it is filled.\n\n## Behavior contract\n\n### Constants\n\n- `REF_PREFIX = \"refs/roast/snapshots/\"`. Prune only ever deletes refs under\n  this prefix.\n- Default ref retention: 30 days (`720h`). Tighter than any artifact\n  retention because the refs live in the user's repository.\n- Snapshot commit identity (environment added to `commit-tree` only):\n  `GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=roast`, `GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=roast@localhost`,\n  `GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=roast`, `GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=roast@localhost`.\n  Capture must not fail on a machine with no `user.email` and must not\n  attribute Roast's object to the user. Dates are not overridden (an\n  inherited `GIT_COMMITTER_DATE` would apply).\n- Commit message: `roast run <run-id>`.\n- `file_modes` string recorded in `meta.json`, verbatim:\n  `regular and executable files plus symlinks; empty directories are not\n  represented` (one line, single space where wrapped here).\n- Temp directory prefixes (under `os.tmpdir()`): `roast-snapshot-`\n  (capture), `roast-pin-` (pin), `roast-checkout-` (checkout default\n  destination).\n\n### Secret denylist\n\nDefault patterns, in this order:\n\n```\n.env*  *.pem  *_rsa  *.key  creds*\nid_rsa*  id_dsa*  id_ecdsa*  id_ed25519*  id_xmss*\ncredentials.json  .npmrc  .pypirc  .netrc  _netrc  .pgpass\n.git-credentials  *.p12  *.pfx\n```\n\n`denylisted(path)`:\n\n1. Lower-case the path and every pattern (case-insensitive file systems\n   open `.ENV` as `.env`).\n2. A path matches if any pattern matches either its basename or the full\n   repo-relative path (`/`-separated), using Go `filepath.Match`\n   semantics: `*` matches any run of characters except `/` (a leading dot\n   is not special), `?` matches one character except `/`, `[...]` is a\n   character class (`^` negates, ranges `a-z`), `\\` escapes the next\n   character. A malformed pattern never matches (the error is swallowed).\n\nSSH keys are matched by key-type prefix so suffixed names\n(`id_ed25519_github`, `id_rsa_work`, `.pub` files) are covered; there is no\nbare `id_*` (it would drop `id_map.go`).\n\n`denylistedUntracked(repo, patterns?)` returns the untracked, non-ignored\npaths matching the denylist, sorted by byte order:\n\n- `patterns` omitted (Go `nil`) means the default list. An explicitly empty\n  list disables filtering and returns `[]` without running Git. No CLI path\n  passes a custom list; it exists for tests.\n- Runs `git -C <repo> ls-files --others --exclude-standard -z`, splits on\n  NUL, drops empty entries, keeps matches.\n- Tracked files are never denylisted here: their content is already in the\n  repository's history.\n\n### Git invocation rules\n\nEvery Git call is `git -C <dir> <args...>` via `execFile`:\n\n- The child inherits the full process environment; per-call extra\n  variables are layered on top (so a per-call `GIT_INDEX_FILE` wins over an\n  inherited one). Calls without extras inherit `GIT_INDEX_FILE` unchanged,\n  except that restore calls clear it (see Restore, deliberate change 12).\n- The result is stdout with leading and trailing whitespace trimmed.\n- On a non-zero exit the error carries the exit status and, when present,\n  trimmed stderr (`<status>: <stderr>`); Git puts every useful message on\n  stderr.\n- Output can be large (`ls-files --others` on a big tree): the runner must\n  not truncate at Node's default `maxBuffer`.\n\n### Live index resolution\n\nThe index a review reads, and therefore the one a snapshot copies:\n\n1. If `GIT_INDEX_FILE` is set in the process environment: that path; a\n   relative value is resolved against the repository toplevel (`repo`).\n   Under the native pre-commit gate Git points it at the temporary index\n   holding exactly the commit's content, which is what `--staged` reviews.\n2. Otherwise `<absolute-git-dir>/index`, where the Git dir comes from\n   `git -C <repo> rev-parse --absolute-git-dir` (per-worktree Git dir for a\n   linked worktree).\n\nCopying the index: copy the file byte for byte to the destination, created\nwith mode `0600` (truncating). A missing source index is not an error and\nnothing is written (Git treats an absent `GIT_INDEX_FILE` as an empty\nindex). Any other read error is `read <src>: <err>`.\n\n### Pin the worktree (`pinWorktree(repo, denylist?)`)\n\nWrites the working tree, as a review sees it, into one tree object. Needs\nno HEAD, writes no ref and no commit, never writes the live index. The\nwritten objects are unreferenced (collectable by `git gc`) until a capture\nanchors them.\n\n1. `git -C <repo> rev-parse --absolute-git-dir`; failure ->\n   `resolve git dir: <err>`.\n2. Make a temp dir `roast-pin-*`; always removed on exit (success or\n   failure).\n3. Copy the live index to `<tmp>/worktree`.\n4. `excluded = denylistedUntracked(repo, denylist)`.\n5. With `GIT_INDEX_FILE=<tmp>/worktree`:\n   `git -C <repo> add -A -- . :(exclude,literal)<p1> :(exclude,literal)<p2>\n   ...` (one exclude per excluded path, in sorted order). The positive `.`\n   pathspec is required (an exclude-only pathspec matches nothing and\n   errors). `literal` stops metacharacters in a filename (for example a\n   file literally named `*.key`) from widening the exclusion. Failure ->\n   `git add -A (snapshot index): <err>`.\n6. With the same `GIT_INDEX_FILE`: `git -C <repo> write-tree`; failure ->\n   `write-tree (worktree): <err>`.\n7. Return `{tree, excluded}`.\n\nBecause `add -A` runs on a private copy, an unmerged (conflicted) live\nindex is resolved in the copy rather than refused, and the live index is\nuntouched.\n\n### Capture (`capture(repo, opts)`)\n\nOptions: `runId` (names the ref), `headOid`, `baseOid`, `denylist?`,\n`indexTree?` (an index tree already pinned by the caller), `worktree?` (a\npin already taken by the caller). Returns the snapshot identity or throws;\nit never returns a partial snapshot.\n\n1. Empty `runId` -> error `snapshot: empty run id`.\n2. Empty `headOid` -> error `repository has no commits; an unborn HEAD\n   cannot be restored, so no replayable snapshot is possible`. (Restore\n   detaches at the original HEAD; without one the only commit-ish is the\n   synthetic composite, a baseline that never existed.)\n3. `git -C <repo> rev-parse --absolute-git-dir`; failure ->\n   `resolve git dir: <err>`.\n4. Make temp dir `roast-snapshot-*`; always removed on exit.\n5. Index tree: if `opts.indexTree` is non-empty use it verbatim. Otherwise\n   copy the live index to `<tmp>/index` and run `git -C <repo> write-tree`\n   with `GIT_INDEX_FILE=<tmp>/index`; failure ->\n   `write-tree (index): <err>`. An unmerged index makes `write-tree`\n   refuse, so capture fails.\n6. Worktree tree: if `opts.worktree.tree` is non-empty use that pin\n   verbatim (tree and excluded list). Otherwise run steps 3-7 of the pin\n   inside this temp dir (copy to `<tmp>/worktree`, denylist, `add -A`,\n   `write-tree`). Reusing caller pins is what guarantees the recorded tree\n   is the reviewed tree.\n7. Composite tree: `git -C <repo> mktree` with stdin exactly\n   `040000 tree <indexTree>\\tindex\\n040000 tree <worktreeTree>\\tworktree\\n`;\n   failure -> `mktree: <err>`.\n8. Commit: `git -C <repo> commit-tree <composite> -p <headOid>\n   [-p <baseOid>] -m \"roast run <runId>\"` with the identity environment.\n   The second parent is added only when `baseOid` is non-empty and differs\n   from `headOid`. Parents keep HEAD and the branch base reachable even\n   after a rebase or branch delete. Failure -> `commit-tree: <err>`.\n9. `git -C <repo> update-ref refs/roast/snapshots/<runId> <commit>` (no\n   old-value check; an existing ref is overwritten). Failure ->\n   `update-ref <ref>: <err>` (for example a read-only `.git`).\n10. Return `{ref, commit, indexTree, worktreeTree, head:headOid,\n    base:baseOid, excluded}`.\n\nThe ref keeps every snapshot object reachable through `git gc --prune=now`.\n\n### Load (`load(repo, runId)`)\n\nThe snapshot is self-describing; nothing else is read.\n\n1. `git -C <repo> rev-parse --verify --quiet <ref>^{commit}`; a failure or\n   empty output -> `no snapshot ref <ref> in <repo> (run predates\n   snapshots, was pruned, or lives in another clone)`.\n2. `rev-parse --verify --quiet <ref>:index`; failure ->\n   `snapshot <ref> has no index/ tree: <err>`.\n3. `rev-parse --verify --quiet <ref>:worktree`; failure ->\n   `snapshot <ref> has no worktree/ tree: <err>`.\n4. `rev-parse --verify --quiet <ref>^1` -> head, `<ref>^2` -> base; each\n   is best-effort (`\"\"` on failure).\n5. `excluded` is not recoverable from the ref and is empty.\n\n### Delete (`deleteSnapshot(repo, runId)`)\n\nEmpty `runId` -> `delete snapshot: empty run id`. Otherwise\n`git -C <repo> update-ref -d refs/roast/snapshots/<runId>`; failure ->\n`delete <ref>: <err>`. Used by the review to avoid leaking an orphan ref\nwhen the run record cannot be initialized.\n\n### Restore (`restore(repo, runId, dir)`)\n\n0. Clear `GIT_INDEX_FILE`: every git call in restore (`worktree add`,\n   `read-tree`, `checkout-index`, and `removeWorktree`) runs with that\n   variable removed from the child environment, so an inherited index\n   (for example inside a hook) never replaces the new worktree's own.\n   Deliberate change 12.\n1. `load(repo, runId)`; its error propagates.\n2. Empty head -> `snapshot <ref> records no HEAD commit; it cannot be\n   restored to a meaningful baseline` (only a hand-made ref can hit this).\n3. `git -C <repo> worktree add --detach --no-checkout <abs> <head>`,\n   where `<abs>` is `dir` resolved against the process working directory\n   once, before any git call (`path.resolve`); every later call in this\n   function uses `<abs>` too. A relative `dir` therefore means the same\n   place for `worktree add`, `read-tree`, and `checkout-index`, from any\n   subdirectory (deliberate change 12);\n   failure -> `git worktree add: <err>`. Git refuses a `dir` that exists\n   and is non-empty; Roast does no check of its own.\n4. In the new worktree (`git -C <abs> ...`):\n   1. `read-tree <worktreeTree>`; failure -> `read-tree (worktree): <err>`.\n   2. `checkout-index -a -f`; failure -> `checkout-index: <err>`. Disk now\n      holds the worktree tree (contents, exec bit, symlinks).\n   3. `read-tree <indexTree>`; failure -> `read-tree (index): <err>`. The\n      index reverts to what was staged while disk is untouched, so paths on\n      disk but absent from the index show as untracked, deleted files show\n      as deleted, and staged versus unstaged edits are distinguished as\n      they were.\n   On any failure in step 4, call `removeWorktree(repo, abs)` (result\n   ignored) before rethrowing, so no half-built worktree is left\n   registered.\n5. Return the loaded identity.\n\nThe restored status equals the original except for denylisted files,\nignored files, and empty directories.\n\nSubmodules (gitlinks) in a snapshot are unsupported and documented as\nsuch: a gitlink entry is not restored as a populated submodule, and no\nbehavior is specified or tested for it.\n\n### Remove worktree (`removeWorktree(repo, dir)`)\n\n`git -C <repo> worktree remove --force <dir>`. If that fails, remove `dir`\nrecursively (errors ignored) and run `git -C <repo> worktree prune`; only\nif the prune also fails, return `worktree remove <dir>: <remove err>`.\n\n### Prune (`prune(repo, olderThanMs, dryRun)`)\n\n1. `olderThan <= 0` -> use the default retention (30 days).\n2. `git -C <repo> for-each-ref\n   --format=%(refname)%09%(committerdate:unix) refs/roast/snapshots/`;\n   failure -> `for-each-ref refs/roast/snapshots/: <err>`.\n3. Process lines in Git's output order (sorted by refname). Skip a line\n   that is blank, has no tab, whose name lacks the prefix, or whose\n   timestamp (trimmed) is not a base-10 integer (for example a ref pointing\n   at a non-commit, whose committer date is empty). Refs of unknown age are\n   never deleted.\n4. `age = now - timestamp`. Skip when `age < olderThan` (a ref exactly at\n   the threshold is pruned).\n5. Otherwise, unless `dryRun`, run `git -C <repo> update-ref -d <ref>`; on\n   failure stop immediately and throw `delete <ref>: <err>` (refs already\n   deleted stay deleted). Each considered ref yields\n   `{ref, runId:<ref minus prefix>, ageMs, deleted}`.\n\nPrune touches only refs. It never removes run records under\n`.roast/runs/` and never runs `git gc`; the unreferenced objects become\ncollectable by Git's own gc.\n\n### Helper lookups\n\n- `headOid(repo)` = `revOid(repo, \"HEAD\")`.\n- `revOid(repo, rev)`: `git -C <repo> rev-parse --verify --quiet\n  <rev>^{commit}`, `\"\"` on failure.\n- `treeOid(repo, rev)`: same with `<rev>^{tree}`, `\"\"` on failure.\n\n### When a review takes snapshots\n\nConfig: `review.snapshot` in `.roast.json`, an optional boolean. Absent or\n`true` means capture; `false` disables capture. There is no environment\nvariable or flag (a `ROAST_SNAPSHOT` variable from older versions has no\neffect). The `init` starter shows it commented out as `\"snapshot\": true`\ninside the `review` block. Schema description: `Record a Git snapshot of\neach review for replay. Default true.`\n\nSequence inside `review`, after trust and config loading:\n\n1. With `--staged`, the context area pins the index tree first\n   (`PinIndexTree`, same live-index rule and copy-then-`write-tree`); its\n   OID is passed to capture as `indexTree`. Without `--staged`,\n   `indexTree` is empty and capture copies the index itself.\n2. Scope is built. An empty scope produces the no-diff report and returns:\n   no run id, no pin, no snapshot.\n3. A run id is minted (records area, `<YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ>-<8 hex>`); the\n   snapshot ref uses the same id.\n4. If the scope is not staged, `pinWorktree(repo)` runs regardless of the\n   setting. On failure the review logs to stderr `roast: could not pin the\n   working tree (<err>); reading it live with .git, ignored, and\n   denylisted paths blocked` and continues with an empty pin; on success\n   the pinned tree becomes the tree every reader uses. A staged scope takes\n   no pin here (capture computes the worktree half itself).\n5. Snapshot capture (`captureSnapshot`), producing the `snapshot` block and\n   caveat strings:\n   - Disabled: `{captured:false, reason:\"disabled by review.snapshot\"}`,\n     caveat `Git snapshot capture was disabled; this run cannot be\n     replayed.` No Git commands, no ref.\n   - `head` = scope's head OID if set, else `headOid(repo)`.\n   - `base` = scope's base OID if set, else `revOid(repo, scope.base)` when\n     the scope has a base rev name, else `\"\"`.\n   - `capture(repo, {runId, headOid:head, baseOid:base, indexTree,\n     worktree:pin})`.\n   - Capture error: `{captured:false, reason:<err message>, head, base}`\n     (head/base omitted when empty), caveat `Git snapshot capture failed:\n     <err message>`. The review continues; capture failure never aborts it.\n   - Success: `diff_pair` is `head..index` for a staged scope, else `head`\n     for a survey (repo) scope, else `head..worktree` when the scope has no\n     base, else `base..worktree`. `base_tree` = `treeOid(repo, source)`\n     where source is `head` for the three `head*` pairs and `base` for\n     `base..worktree`; omitted when the source or the lookup is empty.\n     Caveat `snapshot excluded <N> denylisted untracked path(s)` when the\n     excluded list is non-empty.\n6. If captured, the ref is deleted (`deleteSnapshot`, errors ignored) on\n   every exit path until the run record is initialized (`meta.json` plus\n   the initial artifacts written). Any usage error in between (invalid\n   `--mode`, prompt over the byte ceiling, a failed record write) therefore\n   leaves no orphan ref. After initialization the ref is durable, even if\n   inference later fails or is cancelled.\n7. `meta.json` `scope.base_oid` / `scope.head_oid` fall back to the\n   snapshot block's `base` / `head` when the scope did not pin them.\n8. The caveats go into `meta.json` `caveats`.\n\n### `snapshot` block in `meta.json`\n\nKey order as written by Go; every key except `captured` is omitted when\nempty:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"captured\": true,\n  \"reason\": \"...\",\n  \"ref\": \"refs/roast/snapshots/<run-id>\",\n  \"commit\": \"<oid>\",\n  \"index_tree\": \"<oid>\",\n  \"worktree_tree\": \"<oid>\",\n  \"base_tree\": \"<oid>\",\n  \"head\": \"<oid>\",\n  \"base\": \"<oid>\",\n  \"diff_pair\": \"head..index|head|head..worktree|base..worktree\",\n  \"file_modes\": \"regular and executable files plus symlinks; ...\",\n  \"excluded_paths\": [\"deploy.pem\"]\n}\n```\n\n`reason` appears only when `captured` is false. Snapshot objects are never\ncopied into the run directory or the run bundle; they live in the\nrepository's object store.\n\n### `npx roast runs checkout <run-id> [dir]`\n\n- Arguments: 1 or 2 positionals; any other count is a usage error (exit 2).\n  No flags.\n- Reads the run's `meta.json` through the records area (`readMeta`), which\n  resolves `<run-id>` as a unique prefix of a run directory name under the\n  current Git toplevel's `.roast/runs/`. Any failure (invalid id, outside a\n  repository, no match, ambiguous prefix, missing or unparsable\n  `meta.json`) is a usage error, exit 2, with the records-area message.\n- `snapshot.captured` false -> usage error, exit 2:\n  `run <run-id> has no Git snapshot (<reason>)`, where `<run-id>` is the\n  full id from `meta.json` and `<reason>` may be empty.\n- Destination: `dir` if given, resolved against the working directory\n  once (`path.resolve`) and that absolute path is used by every git call\n  and in the printed output (deliberate change 12); otherwise\n  `<mkdtemp(os.tmpdir()/roast-checkout-)>/<run-id>`. The temp\n  parent is never cleaned up by Roast. A `mkdtemp` failure is a non-usage\n  error (exit 4).\n- `restore(meta.repo, meta.run_id, dest)`, using the repository path\n  recorded in `meta.json`, not the current directory. Failure -> exit 4,\n  message `restore run <run-id>: <err>`.\n- On success: stderr `roast: remove with: git -C <repo> worktree remove\n  --force <dest>` and stdout `<dest>\\n`; exit 0. Stdout carries only the\n  path, so `cd \"$(npx roast runs checkout <id>)\"` works.\n\nErrors print as `roast: <message>` on stderr (or the JSON error shape when\n`--error-format json` applies; CLI area), with exit codes from the shared\nmapping: usage error -> 2, any other error -> 4.\n\n### `npx roast runs prune [--older-than <duration>] [--dry-run]`\n\n- No positionals (any -> usage error, exit 2).\n- `--older-than`: Go duration (`720h`, `1h30m`, `90s`, negative allowed),\n  default `720h0m0s` (30 days). An unparsable value is a flag error, exit\n  2. Zero or negative falls back to 30 days inside prune.\n- `--dry-run`: boolean, default false; lists without deleting.\n- Repository: the toplevel of the current directory\n  (`git rev-parse --show-toplevel`); outside a repository -> usage error\n  `not inside a git repository`, exit 2. Refs are shared by every linked\n  worktree of the clone.\n- Prune error (listing or a delete failing) -> exit 4; nothing is printed\n  to stdout, even for refs deleted before the failure.\n- No refs selected -> stderr `roast: no snapshot refs older than <d>`,\n  where `<d>` is the flag value formatted as a Go duration (the default\n  prints `720h0m0s`; an explicit `0` prints `0s` although 30 days was\n  applied). This is kept as Go parity. Exit 0.\n- Otherwise one stdout line per ref, in for-each-ref order:\n  `deleted <ref> (age <age>)`, or `would delete <ref> (age <age>)` with\n  `--dry-run`. `<age>` is the age rounded to the nearest hour (half away\n  from zero) and formatted as a Go duration, so it is always `<N>h0m0s`, or\n  `0s` when it rounds to zero. Exit 0.\n\nGo duration formatting needed here (shared duration helper): zero is `0s`;\na whole number of hours `N > 0` is `<N>h0m0s`; the general form is\n`[-][<h>h][<m>m]<s>[.<frac>]s` with hours and minutes present once a larger\nunit is.\n\n## TypeScript module design\n\n`src/snapshot/`:\n\n- `denylist.ts`\n  - `export const DEFAULT_DENYLIST:readonly string[]`\n  - `export function goPathMatch(pattern:string, name:string):boolean`\n    (Go `filepath.Match` port; malformed pattern -> `false`)\n  - `export function denylisted(path:string,\n    patterns?:readonly string[]):boolean`\n  - `export async function denylistedUntracked(repo:string,\n    opts?:{patterns?:readonly string[], signal?:AbortSignal})\n    :Promise<string[]>`\n- `snapshot.ts`\n  - `export const REF_PREFIX = 'refs/roast/snapshots/'`\n  - `export const DEFAULT_REF_RETENTION_MS = 30 * 24 * 3600 * 1000`\n  - `export interface Pin { tree:string; excluded:string[] }`\n  - `export interface Snapshot { ref:string; commit:string;\n    indexTree:string; worktreeTree:string; head:string; base:string;\n    excluded:string[] }`\n  - `export interface CaptureOptions { runId:string; headOid:string;\n    baseOid:string; denylist?:readonly string[]; indexTree?:string;\n    worktree?:Pin; signal?:AbortSignal }`\n  - `export function liveIndexPath(repo:string, gitDir:string,\n    env:NodeJS.ProcessEnv):string`\n  - `export async function pinWorktree(repo:string,\n    opts?:{denylist?:readonly string[], signal?:AbortSignal}):Promise<Pin>`\n  - `export async function capture(repo:string,\n    opts:CaptureOptions):Promise<Snapshot>`\n  - `export async function load(repo:string, runId:string,\n    signal?:AbortSignal):Promise<Snapshot>`\n  - `export async function deleteSnapshot(repo:string,\n    runId:string):Promise<void>`\n  - `export async function restore(repo:string, runId:string,\n    dir:string, signal?:AbortSignal):Promise<Snapshot>`\n  - `export async function removeWorktree(repo:string,\n    dir:string):Promise<void>`\n  - `export interface Pruned { ref:string; runId:string; ageMs:number;\n    deleted:boolean }`\n  - `export async function prune(repo:string, olderThanMs:number,\n    dryRun:boolean, now?:() => number):Promise<Pruned[]>`\n  - `export async function headOid(repo:string):Promise<string>`,\n    `revOid(repo, rev)`, `treeOid(repo, rev)`\n- `meta.ts` (pure, no I/O besides the injected lookups)\n  - `export interface SnapshotMeta` matching the JSON block above, with\n    optional fields left `undefined` so `JSON.stringify` omits them; key\n    insertion order follows the JSON block.\n  - `export async function captureSnapshot(input:{enabled:boolean;\n    repo:string; runId:string; scope:ScopeLike; indexTree:string;\n    pin:Pin|null}):Promise<{meta:SnapshotMeta; caveats:string[]}>`\n\n`src/cli/runs.ts` adds `checkout` and `prune` subcommands (the `runs` list\nand `show` belong to the records/CLI areas).\n\nDependencies:\n\n- Shared Git runner (scope area): `git(dir, args, {env?, input?, signal?})`\n  returning trimmed stdout and throwing an error with status plus trimmed\n  stderr; inherits `process.env`, merges extras; unbounded output.\n- Records area: `readMeta(prefix)`, run id, `meta.json` writing.\n- Scope area: `gitRoot(start)` for prune; `Scope` fields `headOid`,\n  `baseOid`, `base`, `staged`, `survey`; scope and context areas import\n  `denylisted` / `denylistedUntracked` from here.\n- Context area: `pinIndexTree` (staged index pin), pinned-tree readers.\n- Config area: `review.snapshot` (absent means `true`).\n- CLI area: usage error type, exit code mapping, Go duration parse/format\n  helpers, `textsafe`-sanitized error printing.\n- Temp directories: `fs.mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(), prefix))`, removed\n  with `fs.rm(dir, {recursive:true, force:true})` in `finally`.\n- Debug tracing: namespace `roast:snapshot` (Git subcommand names and\n  refs only; no file contents).\n\n## Deliberate changes\n\n- Debug tracing via `@substrate-system/debug` under `roast:snapshot`\n  (decision 5).\n- Windows is unsupported (decision 2), so path handling assumes `/`.\n- Relative `runs checkout` directory is resolved against the working\n  directory once, used by every git call (spec.md change 12).\n- `restore` clears an inherited `GIT_INDEX_FILE` (spec.md change 12).\n- Otherwise none: ref names, Git commands, `meta.json` fields, command\n  output, and exit codes match Go.\n\n## Test plan\n\nLower seam (`src/snapshot/` against real temp Git repositories; each test\ncreates its own repo with a fixed author identity). A shared \"messy repo\"\nfixture holds: a clean committed file, a staged modification, an unstaged\nmodification, an unstaged deletion, an untracked file, an untracked file\nwith the exec bit, an untracked symlink, a `.gitignore`d secret, and an\nuntracked `deploy.pem`.\n\n1. Round trip fidelity: capture leaves `git status --porcelain` of the\n   source unchanged; after restore, `git diff --cached HEAD` and\n   `git diff HEAD` are byte-identical to the original, status matches\n   except the denylisted file, untracked content is identical, the exec bit\n   survives, the symlink is still a symlink; `excluded` is `[deploy.pem]`\n   (TestRoundTrip).\n2. Secret hygiene: the worktree tree omits the ignored secret and\n   `deploy.pem`, includes `untracked.go`; `deploy.pem` stays on disk\n   (TestSecretHygiene).\n3. Literal pathspec: an untracked file literally named `*.key` is excluded\n   while `keep.go` is captured (TestDenylistLiteralPathspec).\n4. Denylist matching: listed credential names match, including nested,\n   upper-case, and suffixed SSH key names; `main.go`, `credentials.go`,\n   `npmrc.go`, `README.md`, `id_map.go`, `internal/id_test.go`,\n   `identity.go` do not (TestDenylistCoversCredentialFiles). Pure unit\n   test; also cover `goPathMatch` for `*`, `?`, classes, escapes.\n5. Capture fails (no ref written) on an unmerged index\n   (TestCaptureUnmergedIndexFails).\n6. Capture refuses an unborn HEAD (TestCaptureRefusesUnbornHead).\n7. Parents: first parent is HEAD, second is the base when it differs\n   (TestCaptureRecordsParents).\n8. Snapshot objects survive `git gc --prune=now` (TestSurvivesGC).\n9. Prune: with a fresh snapshot and a ref backdated 60 days (commit-tree\n   with old `GIT_AUTHOR_DATE` / `GIT_COMMITTER_DATE`), a 30-day dry run\n   reports exactly the old ref, not deleted, and it still exists; a real\n   run deletes it and the fresh one still loads (TestPrune).\n10. `GIT_INDEX_FILE` honored: with the live index staging `a.txt` and an\n    alternate index staging only `b.txt`, the captured index tree reflects\n    the alternate (TestCaptureHonorsGitIndexFile).\n11. Pin reuse: a pin and the capture given that pin record the same tree\n    (TestPinWorktreeMatchesCapture).\n12. Pin works without HEAD (TestPinWorktreeWithoutHead) and with an\n    unmerged live index, leaving the live index untouched\n    (TestPinWorktreeUnmergedIndex).\n\nCLI seam (`run(argv, io)` in a temp repo with an approved config and the\nfake inference server):\n\n13. `runs checkout` with 0 or 3 positionals exits 2\n    (TestCommandUsageErrorsExitWithUsageCode).\n14. `runs prune --dry-run` in a repository exits 0\n    (TestRunsLocalSubcommandsStillWork).\n15. After a review, `runs checkout <id> <dest>` exits 0 and materializes\n    the reviewed file in `dest`; clean up with `git worktree remove\n    --force` (TestFinderVerifierPipelineWithMockEndpoint). Also: a\n    relative `dest` run from a subdirectory of the repository succeeds and\n    lands under that subdirectory; with `GIT_INDEX_FILE` set to a bogus\n    path in the environment, restore still succeeds and the new\n    worktree's index is its own.\n16. Default config records `snapshot.captured: true` and a ref under\n    `refs/roast/snapshots/`; `review.snapshot: false` records\n    `captured: false` and writes no refs\n    (TestReviewSnapshotSettingInProjectConfig).\n17. With no HEAD, or with `review.snapshot: false`, the review still reads\n    one pinned tree rather than the live worktree\n    (TestReviewPinsCorpusWithoutSnapshot).\n18. `ROAST_SNAPSHOT=0` in the environment does not disable capture\n    (TestRemovedEnvironmentVariablesHaveNoEffect).\n\nNew tests worth adding (behavior the Go code implements but tests do not\npin): checkout of a run whose snapshot was not captured exits 2; prune\noutput lines on stdout and the empty-result message exit 0; a review that\nfails with a usage error after capture (for example invalid `--mode`)\nleaves no ref.\n\n## Go baseline references\n\nAt commit `cd08f10`:\n\n- `internal/snapshot/snapshot.go`\n- `internal/snapshot/snapshot_test.go`, `internal/snapshot/pin_test.go`\n- `internal/cli/runs.go` (`newRunsCheckoutCommand`,\n  `newRunsPruneCommand`)\n- `internal/cli/cli.go` (review sequence around `snapshot.PinWorktree`,\n  `captureSnapshot`, `snapshotDurable`; `exitCode`)\n- `internal/context/tree.go` (`PinIndexTree`)\n- `i","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-24-runs-checkout-and-prune/0de572ae-0eae-4979-b0e7-cefd41e0bbfc/tool-results/b2gczd9rb.txt","persistedOutputSize":46482},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Cu7EHTehF8djaXwhDsvMKA","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLxpr7qeyAiCvFuWQ5yx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MfwjumzURgMDrg4QFkynkq","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-24-runs-checkout-and-prune/0de572ae-0eae-4979-b0e7-cefd41e0bbfc/tool-results/b2gczd9rb.txt"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":940,"cache_read_input_tokens":55844,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":940},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0de572ae-0eae-4979-b0e7-cefd41e0bbfc","uuid":"538bae81-96a7-4b1a-8f4a-eadac2d4c065","timestamp":"2026-11-24T02:27:43.757Z","request_id":"req_011ChLxpqwuySdTahoQLrZeF","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01MfwjumzURgMDrg4QFkynkq":{"file_path":"/Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-24-runs-checkout-and-prune/0de572ae-0eae-4979-b0e7-cefd41e0bbfc/tool-results/b2gczd9rb.txt"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MfwjumzURgMDrg4QFkynkq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Area 11: Review snapshots, `runs checkout`, `runs prune`\n2\t\n3\t## Purpose\n4\t\n5\tA review snapshot pins exactly what one review read, as ordinary Git\n6\tobjects in the reviewed repository's own object store, so a run record can\n7\tlater be replayed or inspected against the same bytes. It captures the\n8\tindex (what was staged) and the worktree (tracked files plus untracked,\n9\tnon-ignored files, including uncommitted edits and deletions) as two tree\n10\tobjects, joins them in one composite tree, anchors that tree with a commit,\n11\tand points a private ref at the commit:\n12\t\n13\t```\n14\trefs/roast/snapshots/<run-id>  ->  commit \"roast run <run-id>\"\n15\t                                     parents: HEAD [, base]\n16\t                                     tree:\n17\t                                       index/     (index tree)\n18\t                                       worktree/  (worktree tree)\n19\t```\n20\t\n21\tThe live index and the user's working tree are never written. Three things\n22\tare deliberately not captured: `.gitignore`d files (`git add -A` skips them,\n23\tmatching Roast's own scope rules), empty directories (Git cannot represent\n24\tthem), and untracked, non-ignored files whose names match the secret\n25\tdenylist (recorded in the run record as `excluded_paths`).\n26\t\n27\tThe same package provides the \"pin\": the worktree half alone, written as an\n28\tunreferenced tree object before inference starts, so every reader in a\n29\treview (grounding, `read_file`, verifier context) sees one immutable tree\n30\teven when no replayable snapshot can be recorded. The secret denylist is\n31\talso owned here and consumed by the scope and context areas.\n32\t\n33\tTwo `runs` subcommands operate on snapshots:\n34\t\n35\t- `npx roast runs checkout <run-id> [dir]` materializes a run's snapshot\n36\t  into a detached linked worktree whose `git status`, `git diff HEAD`, and\n37\t  `git diff --cached HEAD` reproduce the original state.\n38\t- `npx roast runs prune` deletes aged refs under `refs/roast/snapshots/`.\n39\t\n40\tRun record layout and `meta.json` as a whole belong to the records area;\n41\tthis file specifies only the `snapshot` block and how it is filled.\n42\t\n43\t## Behavior contract\n44\t\n45\t### Constants\n46\t\n47\t- `REF_PREFIX = \"refs/roast/snapshots/\"`. Prune only ever deletes refs under\n48\t  this prefix.\n49\t- Default ref retention: 30 days (`720h`). Tighter than any artifact\n50\t  retention because the refs live in the user's repository.\n51\t- Snapshot commit identity (environment added to `commit-tree` only):\n52\t  `GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=roast`, `GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=roast@localhost`,\n53\t  `GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=roast`, `GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=roast@localhost`.\n54\t  Capture must not fail on a machine with no `user.email` and must not\n55\t  attribute Roast's object to the user. Dates are not overridden (an\n56\t  inherited `GIT_COMMITTER_DATE` would apply).\n57\t- Commit message: `roast run <run-id>`.\n58\t- `file_modes` string recorded in `meta.json`, verbatim:\n59\t  `regular and executable files plus symlinks; empty directories are not\n60\t  represented` (one line, single space where wrapped here).\n61\t- Temp directory prefixes (under `os.tmpdir()`): `roast-snapshot-`\n62\t  (capture), `roast-pin-` (pin), `roast-checkout-` (checkout default\n63\t  destination).\n64\t\n65\t### Secret denylist\n66\t\n67\tDefault patterns, in this order:\n68\t\n69\t```\n70\t.env*  *.pem  *_rsa  *.key  creds*\n71\tid_rsa*  id_dsa*  id_ecdsa*  id_ed25519*  id_xmss*\n72\tcredentials.json  .npmrc  .pypirc  .netrc  _netrc  .pgpass\n73\t.git-credentials  *.p12  *.pfx\n74\t```\n75\t\n76\t`denylisted(path)`:\n77\t\n78\t1. Lower-case the path and every pattern (case-insensitive file systems\n79\t   open `.ENV` as `.env`).\n80\t2. A path matches if any pattern matches either its basename or the full\n81\t   repo-relative path (`/`-separated), using Go `filepath.Match`\n82\t   semantics: `*` matches any run of characters except `/` (a leading dot\n83\t   is not special), `?` matches one character except `/`, `[...]` is a\n84\t   character class (`^` negates, ranges `a-z`), `\\` escapes the next\n85\t   character. A malformed pattern never matches (the error is swallowed).\n86\t\n87\tSSH keys are matched by key-type prefix so suffixed names\n88\t(`id_ed25519_github`, `id_rsa_work`, `.pub` files) are covered; there is no\n89\tbare `id_*` (it would drop `id_map.go`).\n90\t\n91\t`denylistedUntracked(repo, patterns?)` returns the untracked, non-ignored\n92\tpaths matching the denylist, sorted by byte order:\n93\t\n94\t- `patterns` omitted (Go `nil`) means the default list. An explicitly empty\n95\t  list disables filtering and returns `[]` without running Git. No CLI path\n96\t  passes a custom list; it exists for tests.\n97\t- Runs `git -C <repo> ls-files --others --exclude-standard -z`, splits on\n98\t  NUL, drops empty entries, keeps matches.\n99\t- Tracked files are never denylisted here: their content is already in the\n100\t  repository's history.\n101\t\n102\t### Git invocation rules\n103\t\n104\tEvery Git call is `git -C <dir> <args...>` via `execFile`:\n105\t\n106\t- The child inherits the full process environment; per-call extra\n107\t  variables are layered on top (so a per-call `GIT_INDEX_FILE` wins over an\n108\t  inherited one). Calls without extras inherit `GIT_INDEX_FILE` unchanged,\n109\t  except that restore calls clear it (see Restore, deliberate change 12).\n110\t- The result is stdout with leading and trailing whitespace trimmed.\n111\t- On a non-zero exit the error carries the exit status and, when present,\n112\t  trimmed stderr (`<status>: <stderr>`); Git puts every useful message on\n113\t  stderr.\n114\t- Output can be large (`ls-files --others` on a big tree): the runner must\n115\t  not truncate at Node's default `maxBuffer`.\n116\t\n117\t### Live index resolution\n118\t\n119\tThe index a review reads, and therefore the one a snapshot copies:\n120\t\n121\t1. If `GIT_INDEX_FILE` is set in the process environment: that path; a\n122\t   relative value is resolved against the repository toplevel (`repo`).\n123\t   Under the native pre-commit gate Git points it at the temporary index\n124\t   holding exactly the commit's content, which is what `--staged` reviews.\n125\t2. Otherwise `<absolute-git-dir>/index`, where the Git dir comes from\n126\t   `git -C <repo> rev-parse --absolute-git-dir` (per-worktree Git dir for a\n127\t   linked worktree).\n128\t\n129\tCopying the index: copy the file byte for byte to the destination, created\n130\twith mode `0600` (truncating). A missing source index is not an error and\n131\tnothing is written (Git treats an absent `GIT_INDEX_FILE` as an empty\n132\tindex). Any other read error is `read <src>: <err>`.\n133\t\n134\t### Pin the worktree (`pinWorktree(repo, denylist?)`)\n135\t\n136\tWrites the working tree, as a review sees it, into one tree object. Needs\n137\tno HEAD, writes no ref and no commit, never writes the live index. The\n138\twritten objects are unreferenced (collectable by `git gc`) until a capture\n139\tanchors them.\n140\t\n141\t1. `git -C <repo> rev-parse --absolute-git-dir`; failure ->\n142\t   `resolve git dir: <err>`.\n143\t2. Make a temp dir `roast-pin-*`; always removed on exit (success or\n144\t   failure).\n145\t3. Copy the live index to `<tmp>/worktree`.\n146\t4. `excluded = denylistedUntracked(repo, denylist)`.\n147\t5. With `GIT_INDEX_FILE=<tmp>/worktree`:\n148\t   `git -C <repo> add -A -- . :(exclude,literal)<p1> :(exclude,literal)<p2>\n149\t   ...` (one exclude per excluded path, in sorted order). The positive `.`\n150\t   pathspec is required (an exclude-only pathspec matches nothing and\n151\t   errors). `literal` stops metacharacters in a filename (for example a\n152\t   file literally named `*.key`) from widening the exclusion. Failure ->\n153\t   `git add -A (snapshot index): <err>`.\n154\t6. With the same `GIT_INDEX_FILE`: `git -C <repo> write-tree`; failure ->\n155\t   `write-tree (worktree): <err>`.\n156\t7. Return `{tree, excluded}`.\n157\t\n158\tBecause `add -A` runs on a private copy, an unmerged (conflicted) live\n159\tindex is resolved in the copy rather than refused, and the live index is\n160\tuntouched.\n161\t\n162\t### Capture (`capture(repo, opts)`)\n163\t\n164\tOptions: `runId` (names the ref), `headOid`, `baseOid`, `denylist?`,\n165\t`indexTree?` (an index tree already pinned by the caller), `worktree?` (a\n166\tpin already taken by the caller). Returns the snapshot identity or throws;\n167\tit never returns a partial snapshot.\n168\t\n169\t1. Empty `runId` -> error `snapshot: empty run id`.\n170\t2. Empty `headOid` -> error `repository has no commits; an unborn HEAD\n171\t   cannot be restored, so no replayable snapshot is possible`. (Restore\n172\t   detaches at the original HEAD; without one the only commit-ish is the\n173\t   synthetic composite, a baseline that never existed.)\n174\t3. `git -C <repo> rev-parse --absolute-git-dir`; failure ->\n175\t   `resolve git dir: <err>`.\n176\t4. Make temp dir `roast-snapshot-*`; always removed on exit.\n177\t5. Index tree: if `opts.indexTree` is non-empty use it verbatim. Otherwise\n178\t   copy the live index to `<tmp>/index` and run `git -C <repo> write-tree`\n179\t   with `GIT_INDEX_FILE=<tmp>/index`; failure ->\n180\t   `write-tree (index): <err>`. An unmerged index makes `write-tree`\n181\t   refuse, so capture fails.\n182\t6. Worktree tree: if `opts.worktree.tree` is non-empty use that pin\n183\t   verbatim (tree and excluded list). Otherwise run steps 3-7 of the pin\n184\t   inside this temp dir (copy to `<tmp>/worktree`, denylist, `add -A`,\n185\t   `write-tree`). Reusing caller pins is what guarantees the recorded tree\n186\t   is the reviewed tree.\n187\t7. Composite tree: `git -C <repo> mktree` with stdin exactly\n188\t   `040000 tree <indexTree>\\tindex\\n040000 tree <worktreeTree>\\tworktree\\n`;\n189\t   failure -> `mktree: <err>`.\n190\t8. Commit: `git -C <repo> commit-tree <composite> -p <headOid>\n191\t   [-p <baseOid>] -m \"roast run <runId>\"` with the identity environment.\n192\t   The second parent is added only when `baseOid` is non-empty and differs\n193\t   from `headOid`. Parents keep HEAD and the branch base reachable even\n194\t   after a rebase or branch delete. Failure -> `commit-tree: <err>`.\n195\t9. `git -C <repo> update-ref refs/roast/snapshots/<runId> <commit>` (no\n196\t   old-value check; an existing ref is overwritten). Failure ->\n197\t   `update-ref <ref>: <err>` (for example a read-only `.git`).\n198\t10. Return `{ref, commit, indexTree, worktreeTree, head:headOid,\n199\t    base:baseOid, excluded}`.\n200\t\n201\tThe ref keeps every snapshot object reachable through `git gc --prune=now`.\n202\t\n203\t### Load (`load(repo, runId)`)\n204\t\n205\tThe snapshot is self-describing; nothing else is read.\n206\t\n207\t1. `git -C <repo> rev-parse --verify --quiet <ref>^{commit}`; a failure or\n208\t   empty output -> `no snapshot ref <ref> in <repo> (run predates\n209\t   snapshots, was pruned, or lives in another clone)`.\n210\t2. `rev-parse --verify --quiet <ref>:index`; failure ->\n211\t   `snapshot <ref> has no index/ tree: <err>`.\n212\t3. `rev-parse --verify --quiet <ref>:worktree`; failure ->\n213\t   `snapshot <ref> has no worktree/ tree: <err>`.\n214\t4. `rev-parse --verify --quiet <ref>^1` -> head, `<ref>^2` -> base; each\n215\t   is best-effort (`\"\"` on failure).\n216\t5. `excluded` is not recoverable from the ref and is empty.\n217\t\n218\t### Delete (`deleteSnapshot(repo, runId)`)\n219\t\n220\tEmpty `runId` -> `delete snapshot: empty run id`. Otherwise\n221\t`git -C <repo> update-ref -d refs/roast/snapshots/<runId>`; failure ->\n222\t`delete <ref>: <err>`. Used by the review to avoid leaking an orphan ref\n223\twhen the run record cannot be initialized.\n224\t\n225\t### Restore (`restore(repo, runId, dir)`)\n226\t\n227\t0. Clear `GIT_INDEX_FILE`: every git call in restore (`worktree add`,\n228\t   `read-tree`, `checkout-index`, and `removeWorktree`) runs with that\n229\t   variable removed from the child environment, so an inherited index\n230\t   (for example inside a hook) never replaces the new worktree's own.\n231\t   Deliberate change 12.\n232\t1. `load(repo, runId)`; its error propagates.\n233\t2. Empty head -> `snapshot <ref> records no HEAD commit; it cannot be\n234\t   restored to a meaningful baseline` (only a hand-made ref can hit this).\n235\t3. `git -C <repo> worktree add --detach --no-checkout <abs> <head>`,\n236\t   where `<abs>` is `dir` resolved against the process working directory\n237\t   once, before any git call (`path.resolve`); every later call in this\n238\t   function uses `<abs>` too. A relative `dir` therefore means the same\n239\t   place for `worktree add`, `read-tree`, and `checkout-index`, from any\n240\t   subdirectory (deliberate change 12);\n241\t   failure -> `git worktree add: <err>`. Git refuses a `dir` that exists\n242\t   and is non-empty; Roast does no check of its own.\n243\t4. In the new worktree (`git -C <abs> ...`):\n244\t   1. `read-tree <worktreeTree>`; failure -> `read-tree (worktree): <err>`.\n245\t   2. `checkout-index -a -f`; failure -> `checkout-index: <err>`. Disk now\n246\t      holds the worktree tree (contents, exec bit, symlinks).\n247\t   3. `read-tree <indexTree>`; failure -> `read-tree (index): <err>`. The\n248\t      index reverts to what was staged while disk is untouched, so paths on\n249\t      disk but absent from the index show as untracked, deleted files show\n250\t      as deleted, and staged versus unstaged edits are distinguished as\n251\t      they were.\n252\t   On any failure in step 4, call `removeWorktree(repo, abs)` (result\n253\t   ignored) before rethrowing, so no half-built worktree is left\n254\t   registered.\n255\t5. Return the loaded identity.\n256\t\n257\tThe restored status equals the original except for denylisted files,\n258\tignored files, and empty directories.\n259\t\n260\tSubmodules (gitlinks) in a snapshot are unsupported and documented as\n261\tsuch: a gitlink entry is not restored as a populated submodule, and no\n262\tbehavior is specified or tested for it.\n263\t\n264\t### Remove worktree (`removeWorktree(repo, dir)`)\n265\t\n266\t`git -C <repo> worktree remove --force <dir>`. If that fails, remove `dir`\n267\trecursively (errors ignored) and run `git -C <repo> worktree prune`; only\n268\tif the prune also fails, return `worktree remove <dir>: <remove err>`.\n269\t\n270\t### Prune (`prune(repo, olderThanMs, dryRun)`)\n271\t\n272\t1. `olderThan <= 0` -> use the default retention (30 days).\n273\t2. `git -C <repo> for-each-ref\n274\t   --format=%(refname)%09%(committerdate:unix) refs/roast/snapshots/`;\n275\t   failure -> `for-each-ref refs/roast/snapshots/: <err>`.\n276\t3. Process lines in Git's output order (sorted by refname). Skip a line\n277\t   that is blank, has no tab, whose name lacks the prefix, or whose\n278\t   timestamp (trimmed) is not a base-10 integer (for example a ref pointing\n279\t   at a non-commit, whose committer date is empty). Refs of unknown age are\n280\t   never deleted.\n281\t4. `age = now - timestamp`. Skip when `age < olderThan` (a ref exactly at\n282\t   the threshold is pruned).\n283\t5. Otherwise, unless `dryRun`, run `git -C <repo> update-ref -d <ref>`; on\n284\t   failure stop immediately and throw `delete <ref>: <err>` (refs already\n285\t   deleted stay deleted). Each considered ref yields\n286\t   `{ref, runId:<ref minus prefix>, ageMs, deleted}`.\n287\t\n288\tPrune touches only refs. It never removes run records under\n289\t`.roast/runs/` and never runs `git gc`; the unreferenced objects become\n290\tcollectable by Git's own gc.\n291\t\n292\t### Helper lookups\n293\t\n294\t- `headOid(repo)` = `revOid(repo, \"HEAD\")`.\n295\t- `revOid(repo, rev)`: `git -C <repo> rev-parse --verify --quiet\n296\t  <rev>^{commit}`, `\"\"` on failure.\n297\t- `treeOid(repo, rev)`: same with `<rev>^{tree}`, `\"\"` on failure.\n298\t\n299\t### When a review takes snapshots\n300\t\n301\tConfig: `review.snapshot` in `.roast.json`, an optional boolean. Absent or\n302\t`true` means capture; `false` disables capture. There is no environment\n303\tvariable or flag (a `ROAST_SNAPSHOT` variable from older versions has no\n304\teffect). The `init` starter shows it commented out as `\"snapshot\": true`\n305\tinside the `review` block. Schema description: `Record a Git snapshot of\n306\teach review for replay. Default true.`\n307\t\n308\tSequence inside `review`, after trust and config loading:\n309\t\n310\t1. With `--staged`, the context area pins the index tree first\n311\t   (`PinIndexTree`, same live-index rule and copy-then-`write-tree`); its\n312\t   OID is passed to capture as `indexTree`. Without `--staged`,\n313\t   `indexTree` is empty and capture copies the index itself.\n314\t2. Scope is built. An empty scope produces the no-diff report and returns:\n315\t   no run id, no pin, no snapshot.\n316\t3. A run id is minted (records area, `<YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ>-<8 hex>`); the\n317\t   snapshot ref uses the same id.\n318\t4. If the scope is not staged, `pinWorktree(repo)` runs regardless of the\n319\t   setting. On failure the review logs to stderr `roast: could not pin the\n320\t   working tree (<err>); reading it live with .git, ignored, and\n321\t   denylisted paths blocked` and continues with an empty pin; on success\n322\t   the pinned tree becomes the tree every reader uses. A staged scope takes\n323\t   no pin here (capture computes the worktree half itself).\n324\t5. Snapshot capture (`captureSnapshot`), producing the `snapshot` block and\n325\t   caveat strings:\n326\t   - Disabled: `{captured:false, reason:\"disabled by review.snapshot\"}`,\n327\t     caveat `Git snapshot capture was disabled; this run cannot be\n328\t     replayed.` No Git commands, no ref.\n329\t   - `head` = scope's head OID if set, else `headOid(repo)`.\n330\t   - `base` = scope's base OID if set, else `revOid(repo, scope.base)` when\n331\t     the scope has a base rev name, else `\"\"`.\n332\t   - `capture(repo, {runId, headOid:head, baseOid:base, indexTree,\n333\t     worktree:pin})`.\n334\t   - Capture error: `{captured:false, reason:<err message>, head, base}`\n335\t     (head/base omitted when empty), caveat `Git snapshot capture failed:\n336\t     <err message>`. The review continues; capture failure never aborts it.\n337\t   - Success: `diff_pair` is `head..index` for a staged scope, else `head`\n338\t     for a survey (repo) scope, else `head..worktree` when the scope has no\n339\t     base, else `base..worktree`. `base_tree` = `treeOid(repo, source)`\n340\t     where source is `head` for the three `head*` pairs and `base` for\n341\t     `base..worktree`; omitted when the source or the lookup is empty.\n342\t     Caveat `snapshot excluded <N> denylisted untracked path(s)` when the\n343\t     excluded list is non-empty.\n344\t6. If captured, the ref is deleted (`deleteSnapshot`, errors ignored) on\n345\t   every exit path until the run record is initialized (`meta.json` plus\n346\t   the initial artifacts written). Any usage error in between (invalid\n347\t   `--mode`, prompt over the byte ceiling, a failed record write) therefore\n348\t   leaves no orphan ref. After initialization the ref is durable, even if\n349\t   inference later fails or is cancelled.\n350\t7. `meta.json` `scope.base_oid` / `scope.head_oid` fall back to the\n351\t   snapshot block's `base` / `head` when the scope did not pin them.\n352\t8. The caveats go into `meta.json` `caveats`.\n353\t\n354\t### `snapshot` block in `meta.json`\n355\t\n356\tKey order as written by Go; every key except `captured` is omitted when\n357\tempty:\n358\t\n359\t```json\n360\t{\n361\t  \"captured\": true,\n362\t  \"reason\": \"...\",\n363\t  \"ref\": \"refs/roast/snapshots/<run-id>\",\n364\t  \"commit\": \"<oid>\",\n365\t  \"index_tree\": \"<oid>\",\n366\t  \"worktree_tree\": \"<oid>\",\n367\t  \"base_tree\": \"<oid>\",\n368\t  \"head\": \"<oid>\",\n369\t  \"base\": \"<oid>\",\n370\t  \"diff_pair\": \"head..index|head|head..worktree|base..worktree\",\n371\t  \"file_modes\": \"regular and executable files plus symlinks; ...\",\n372\t  \"excluded_paths\": [\"deploy.pem\"]\n373\t}\n374\t```\n375\t\n376\t`reason` appears only when `captured` is false. Snapshot objects are never\n377\tcopied into the run directory or the run bundle; they live in the\n378\trepository's object store.\n379\t\n380\t### `npx roast runs checkout <run-id> [dir]`\n381\t\n382\t- Arguments: 1 or 2 positionals; any other count is a usage error (exit 2).\n383\t  No flags.\n384\t- Reads the run's `meta.json` through the records area (`readMeta`), which\n385\t  resolves `<run-id>` as a unique prefix of a run directory name under the\n386\t  current Git toplevel's `.roast/runs/`. Any failure (invalid id, outside a\n387\t  repository, no match, ambiguous prefix, missing or unparsable\n388\t  `meta.json`) is a usage error, exit 2, with the records-area message.\n389\t- `snapshot.captured` false -> usage error, exit 2:\n390\t  `run <run-id> has no Git snapshot (<reason>)`, where `<run-id>` is the\n391\t  full id from `meta.json` and `<reason>` may be empty.\n392\t- Destination: `dir` if given, resolved against the working directory\n393\t  once (`path.resolve`) and that absolute path is used by every git call\n394\t  and in the printed output (deliberate change 12); otherwise\n395\t  `<mkdtemp(os.tmpdir()/roast-checkout-)>/<run-id>`. The temp\n396\t  parent is never cleaned up by Roast. A `mkdtemp` failure is a non-usage\n397\t  error (exit 4).\n398\t- `restore(meta.repo, meta.run_id, dest)`, using the repository path\n399\t  recorded in `meta.json`, not the current directory. Failure -> exit 4,\n400\t  message `restore run <run-id>: <err>`.\n401\t- On success: stderr `roast: remove with: git -C <repo> worktree remove\n402\t  --force <dest>` and stdout `<dest>\\n`; exit 0. Stdout carries only the\n403\t  path, so `cd \"$(npx roast runs checkout <id>)\"` works.\n404\t\n405\tErrors print as `roast: <message>` on stderr (or the JSON error shape when\n406\t`--error-format json` applies; CLI area), with exit codes from the shared\n407\tmapping: usage error -> 2, any other error -> 4.\n408\t\n409\t### `npx roast runs prune [--older-than <duration>] [--dry-run]`\n410\t\n411\t- No positionals (any -> usage error, exit 2).\n412\t- `--older-than`: Go duration (`720h`, `1h30m`, `90s`, negative allowed),\n413\t  default `720h0m0s` (30 days). An unparsable value is a flag error, exit\n414\t  2. Zero or negative falls back to 30 days inside prune.\n415\t- `--dry-run`: boolean, default false; lists without deleting.\n416\t- Repository: the toplevel of the current directory\n417\t  (`git rev-parse --show-toplevel`); outside a repository -> usage error\n418\t  `not inside a git repository`, exit 2. Refs are shared by every linked\n419\t  worktree of the clone.\n420\t- Prune error (listing or a delete failing) -> exit 4; nothing is printed\n421\t  to stdout, even for refs deleted before the failure.\n422\t- No refs selected -> stderr `roast: no snapshot refs older than <d>`,\n423\t  where `<d>` is the flag value formatted as a Go duration (the default\n424\t  prints `720h0m0s`; an explicit `0` prints `0s` although 30 days was\n425\t  applied). This is kept as Go parity. Exit 0.\n426\t- Otherwise one stdout line per ref, in for-each-ref order:\n427\t  `deleted <ref> (age <age>)`, or `would delete <ref> (age <age>)` with\n428\t  `--dry-run`. `<age>` is the age rounded to the nearest hour (half away\n429\t  from zero) and formatted as a Go duration, so it is always `<N>h0m0s`, or\n430\t  `0s` when it rounds to zero. Exit 0.\n431\t\n432\tGo duration formatting needed here (shared duration helper): zero is `0s`;\n433\ta whole number of hours `N > 0` is `<N>h0m0s`; the general form is\n434\t`[-][<h>h][<m>m]<s>[.<frac>]s` with hours and minutes present once a larger\n435\tunit is.\n436\t\n437\t## TypeScript module design\n438\t\n439\t`src/snapshot/`:\n440\t\n441\t- `denylist.ts`\n442\t  - `export const DEFAULT_DENYLIST:readonly string[]`\n443\t  - `export function goPathMatch(pattern:string, name:string):boolean`\n444\t    (Go `filepath.Match` port; malformed pattern -> `false`)\n445\t  - `export function denylisted(path:string,\n446\t    patterns?:readonly string[]):boolean`\n447\t  - `export async function denylistedUntracked(repo:string,\n448\t    opts?:{patterns?:readonly string[], signal?:AbortSignal})\n449\t    :Promise<string[]>`\n450\t- `snapshot.ts`\n451\t  - `export const REF_PREFIX = 'refs/roast/snapshots/'`\n452\t  - `export const DEFAULT_REF_RETENTION_MS = 30 * 24 * 3600 * 1000`\n453\t  - `export interface Pin { tree:string; excluded:string[] }`\n454\t  - `export interface Snapshot { ref:string; commit:string;\n455\t    indexTree:string; worktreeTree:string; head:string; base:string;\n456\t    excluded:string[] }`\n457\t  - `export interface CaptureOptions { runId:string; headOid:string;\n458\t    baseOid:string; denylist?:readonly string[]; indexTree?:string;\n459\t    worktree?:Pin; signal?:AbortSignal }`\n460\t  - `export function liveIndexPath(repo:string, gitDir:string,\n461\t    env:NodeJS.ProcessEnv):string`\n462\t  - `export async function pinWorktree(repo:string,\n463\t    opts?:{denylist?:readonly string[], signal?:AbortSignal}):Promise<Pin>`\n464\t  - `export async function capture(repo:string,\n465\t    opts:CaptureOptions):Promise<Snapshot>`\n466\t  - `export async function load(repo:string, runId:string,\n467\t    signal?:AbortSignal):Promise<Snapshot>`\n468\t  - `export async function deleteSnapshot(repo:string,\n469\t    runId:string):Promise<void>`\n470\t  - `export async function restore(repo:string, runId:string,\n471\t    dir:string, signal?:AbortSignal):Promise<Snapshot>`\n472\t  - `export async function removeWorktree(repo:string,\n473\t    dir:string):Promise<void>`\n474\t  - `export interface Pruned { ref:string; runId:string; ageMs:number;\n475\t    deleted:boolean }`\n476\t  - `export async function prune(repo:string, olderThanMs:number,\n477\t    dryRun:boolean, now?:() => number):Promise<Pruned[]>`\n478\t  - `export async function headOid(repo:string):Promise<string>`,\n479\t    `revOid(repo, rev)`, `treeOid(repo, rev)`\n480\t- `meta.ts` (pure, no I/O besides the injected lookups)\n481\t  - `export interface SnapshotMeta` matching the JSON block above, with\n482\t    optional fields left `undefined` so `JSON.stringify` omits them; key\n483\t    insertion order follows the JSON block.\n484\t  - `export async function captureSnapshot(input:{enabled:boolean;\n485\t    repo:string; runId:string; scope:ScopeLike; indexTree:string;\n486\t    pin:Pin|null}):Promise<{meta:SnapshotMeta; caveats:string[]}>`\n487\t\n488\t`src/cli/runs.ts` adds `checkout` and `prune` subcommands (the `runs` list\n489\tand `show` belong to the records/CLI areas).\n490\t\n491\tDependencies:\n492\t\n493\t- Shared Git runner (scope area): `git(dir, args, {env?, input?, signal?})`\n494\t  returning trimmed stdout and throwing an error with status plus trimmed\n495\t  stderr; inherits `process.env`, merges extras; unbounded output.\n496\t- Records area: `readMeta(prefix)`, run id, `meta.json` writing.\n497\t- Scope area: `gitRoot(start)` for prune; `Scope` fields `headOid`,\n498\t  `baseOid`, `base`, `staged`, `survey`; scope and context areas import\n499\t  `denylisted` / `denylistedUntracked` from here.\n500\t- Context area: `pinIndexTree` (staged index pin), pinned-tree readers.\n501\t- Config area: `review.snapshot` (absent means `true`).\n502\t- CLI area: usage error type, exit code mapping, Go duration parse/format\n503\t  helpers, `textsafe`-sanitized error printing.\n504\t- Temp directories: `fs.mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(), prefix))`, removed\n505\t  with `fs.rm(dir, {recursive:true, force:true})` in `finally`.\n506\t- Debug tracing: namespace `roast:snapshot` (Git subcommand names and\n507\t  refs only; no file contents).\n508\t\n509\t## Deliberate changes\n510\t\n511\t- Debug tracing via `@substrate-system/debug` under `roast:snapshot`\n512\t  (decision 5).\n513\t- Windows is unsupported (decision 2), so path handling assumes `/`.\n514\t- Relative `runs checkout` directory is resolved against the working\n515\t  directory once, used by every git call (spec.md change 12).\n516\t- `restore` clears an inherited `GIT_INDEX_FILE` (spec.md change 12).\n517\t- Otherwise none: ref names, Git commands, `meta.json` fields, command\n518\t  output, and exit codes match Go.\n519\t\n520\t## Test plan\n521\t\n522\tLower seam (`src/snapshot/` against real temp Git repositories; each test\n523\tcreates its own repo with a fixed author identity). A shared \"messy repo\"\n524\tfixture holds: a clean committed file, a staged modification, an unstaged\n525\tmodification, an unstaged deletion, an untracked file, an untracked file\n526\twith the exec bit, an untracked symlink, a `.gitignore`d secret, and an\n527\tuntracked `deploy.pem`.\n528\t\n529\t1. Round trip fidelity: capture leaves `git status --porcelain` of the\n530\t   source unchanged; after restore, `git diff --cached HEAD` and\n531\t   `git diff HEAD` are byte-identical to the original, status matches\n532\t   except the denylisted file, untracked content is identical, the exec bit\n533\t   survives, the symlink is still a symlink; `excluded` is `[deploy.pem]`\n534\t   (TestRoundTrip).\n535\t2. Secret hygiene: the worktree tree omits the ignored secret and\n536\t   `deploy.pem`, includes `untracked.go`; `deploy.pem` stays on disk\n537\t   (TestSecretHygiene).\n538\t3. Literal pathspec: an untracked file literally named `*.key` is excluded\n539\t   while `keep.go` is captured (TestDenylistLiteralPathspec).\n540\t4. Denylist matching: listed credential names match, including nested,\n541\t   upper-case, and suffixed SSH key names; `main.go`, `credentials.go`,\n542\t   `npmrc.go`, `README.md`, `id_map.go`, `internal/id_test.go`,\n543\t   `identity.go` do not (TestDenylistCoversCredentialFiles). Pure unit\n544\t   test; also cover `goPathMatch` for `*`, `?`, classes, escapes.\n545\t5. Capture fails (no ref written) on an unmerged index\n546\t   (TestCaptureUnmergedIndexFails).\n547\t6. Capture refuses an unborn HEAD (TestCaptureRefusesUnbornHead).\n548\t7. Parents: first parent is HEAD, second is the base when it differs\n549\t   (TestCaptureRecordsParents).\n550\t8. Snapshot objects survive `git gc --prune=now` (TestSurvivesGC).\n551\t9. Prune: with a fresh snapshot and a ref backdated 60 days (commit-tree\n552\t   with old `GIT_AUTHOR_DATE` / `GIT_COMMITTER_DATE`), a 30-day dry run\n553\t   reports exactly the old ref, not deleted, and it still exists; a real\n554\t   run deletes it and the fresh one still loads (TestPrune).\n555\t10. `GIT_INDEX_FILE` honored: with the live index staging `a.txt` and an\n556\t    alternate index staging only `b.txt`, the captured index tree reflects\n557\t    the alternate (TestCaptureHonorsGitIndexFile).\n558\t11. Pin reuse: a pin and the capture given that pin record the same tree\n559\t    (TestPinWorktreeMatchesCapture).\n560\t12. Pin works without HEAD (TestPinWorktreeWithoutHead) and with an\n561\t    unmerged live index, leaving the live index untouched\n562\t    (TestPinWorktreeUnmergedIndex).\n563\t\n564\tCLI seam (`run(argv, io)` in a temp repo with an approved config and the\n565\tfake inference server):\n566\t\n567\t13. `runs checkout` with 0 or 3 positionals exits 2\n568\t    (TestCommandUsageErrorsExitWithUsageCode).\n569\t14. `runs prune --dry-run` in a repository exits 0\n570\t    (TestRunsLocalSubcommandsStillWork).\n571\t15. After a review, `runs checkout <id> <dest>` exits 0 and materializes\n572\t    the reviewed file in `dest`; clean up with `git worktree remove\n573\t    --force` (TestFinderVerifierPipelineWithMockEndpoint). Also: a\n574\t    relative `dest` run from a subdirectory of the repository succeeds and\n575\t    lands under that subdirectory; with `GIT_INDEX_FILE` set to a bogus\n576\t    path in the environment, restore still succeeds and the new\n577\t    worktree's index is its own.\n578\t16. Default config records `snapshot.captured: true` and a ref under\n579\t    `refs/roast/snapshots/`; `review.snapshot: false` records\n580\t    `captured: false` and writes no refs\n581\t    (TestReviewSnapshotSettingInProjectConfig).\n582\t17. With no HEAD, or with `review.snapshot: false`, the review still reads\n583\t    one pinned tree rather than the live worktree\n584\t    (TestReviewPinsCorpusWithoutSnapshot).\n585\t18. `ROAST_SNAPSHOT=0` in the environment does not disable capture\n586\t    (TestRemovedEnvironmentVariablesHaveNoEffect).\n587\t\n588\tNew tests worth adding (behavior the Go code implements but tests do not\n589\tpin): checkout of a run whose snapshot was not captured exits 2; prune\n590\toutput lines on stdout and the empty-result message exit 0; a review that\n591\tfails with a usage error after capture (for example invalid `--mode`)\n592\tleaves no ref.\n593\t\n594\t## Go baseline references\n595\t\n596\tAt commit `cd08f10`:\n597\t\n598\t- `internal/snapshot/snapshot.go`\n599\t- `internal/snapshot/snapshot_test.go`, `internal/snapshot/pin_test.go`\n600\t- `internal/cli/runs.go` (`newRunsCheckoutCommand`,\n601\t  `newRunsPruneCommand`)\n602\t- `internal/cli/cli.go` (review sequence around `snapshot.PinWorktree`,\n603\t  `captureSnapshot`, `snapshotDurable`; `exitCode`)\n604\t- `internal/context/tree.go` (`PinIndexTree`)\n605\t- `internal/record/meta.go` (`SnapshotMeta`, `ReadMeta`, `RunDir`)\n606\t- `internal/config/config.go` (`Review.Snapshot`, `SnapshotEnabled`),\n607\t  `internal/config/starter.go`, `roast.schema.json`\n608\t- `internal/cli/cli_test.go`, `internal/cli/settings_test.go`,\n609\t  `internal/cli/hardening_test.go`\n610\t\n611\t## Resolved\n612\t\n613\tResolved (maintainer decisions, see spec.md):\n614\t\n615\t1. A relative `runs checkout` dir is resolved against the working\n616\t   directory once and used by every git call (spec.md change 12).\n617\t2. `runs checkout` / restore clears an inherited `GIT_INDEX_FILE` (spec.md\n618\t   change 12).\n619\t3. Submodules (gitlinks) in snapshots are documented as unsupported\n620\t   (spec.md Out of Scope).\n621\t4. Prune's `0s` message when `--older-than` is <= 0 stays as Go parity.\n622\t// Review snapshots (spec 11): the worktree pin, the replayable capture\n623\t// under refs/roast/snapshots/, its deletion, and revision lookups. The\n624\t// live index and the working tree are never written.\n625\timport fs from 'node:fs/promises'\n626\timport os from 'node:os'\n627\timport path from 'node:path'\n628\timport { git } from '../git/git.js'\n629\timport { denylistedUntracked } from './denylist.js'\n630\t\n631\texport const REF_PREFIX = 'refs/roast/snapshots/'\n632\texport const DEFAULT_REF_RETENTION_MS = 30 * 24 * 3600 * 1000\n633\t\n634\t// Added to commit-tree only: capture works without user.email and never\n635\t// attributes Roast's object to the user. Dates are not overridden.\n636\tconst IDENTITY:Record<string, string> = {\n637\t    GIT_AUTHOR_NAME:'roast',\n638\t    GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL:'roast@localhost',\n639\t    GIT_COMMITTER_NAME:'roast',\n640\t    GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL:'roast@localhost',\n641\t}\n642\t\n643\texport interface Pin {\n644\t    tree:string;\n645\t    excluded:string[];\n646\t}\n647\t\n648\texport interface Snapshot {\n649\t    ref:string;\n650\t    commit:string;\n651\t    indexTree:string;\n652\t    worktreeTree:string;\n653\t    head:string;\n654\t    base:string;\n655\t    excluded:string[];\n656\t}\n657\t\n658\texport interface SnapshotOpts {\n659\t    signal?:AbortSignal;\n660\t    // The environment git runs with (and GIT_INDEX_FILE is read from);\n661\t    // process.env when absent.\n662\t    env?:NodeJS.ProcessEnv;\n663\t}\n664\t\n665\texport interface PinOptions extends SnapshotOpts {\n666\t    // Omitted means the default denylist; empty disables filtering.\n667\t    denylist?:readonly string[];\n668\t}\n669\t\n670\texport interface CaptureOptions extends PinOptions {\n671\t    runId:string;\n672\t    headOid:string;\n673\t    baseOid:string;\n674\t    // An index tree already pinned by the caller (staged reviews).\n675\t    indexTree?:string;\n676\t    // A worktree pin already taken by the caller.\n677\t    worktree?:Pin|null;\n678\t}\n679\t\n680\tfunction message (err:unknown):string {\n681\t    return err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)\n682\t}\n683\t\n684\tfunction wrap (prefix:string, err:unknown):Error {\n685\t    return new Error(`${prefix}: ${message(err)}`, { cause:err })\n686\t}\n687\t\n688\tasync function run (\n689\t    repo:string,\n690\t    args:readonly string[],\n691\t    opts:SnapshotOpts,\n692\t    extra?:{ env?:Record<string, string>, input?:string }\n693\t):Promise<string> {\n694\t    const out = await git(repo, args, {\n695\t        signal:opts.signal,\n696\t        env:opts.env,\n697\t        extraEnv:extra?.env,\n698\t        input:extra?.input,\n699\t    })\n700\t    return out.toString('utf8').trim()\n701\t}\n702\t\n703\t// Runs a git step, wrapping a failure with `prefix` unless aborted.\n704\tasync function step (\n705\t    prefix:string,\n706\t    opts:SnapshotOpts,\n707\t    fn:() => Promise<string>\n708\t):Promise<string> {\n709\t    try {\n710\t        return await fn()\n711\t    } catch (err) {\n712\t        opts.signal?.throwIfAborted()\n713\t        throw wrap(prefix, err)\n714\t    }\n715\t}\n716\t\n717\t// The index a review reads: GIT_INDEX_FILE from `env` (relative values\n718\t// against the toplevel `repo`), else `<gitDir>/index`.\n719\texport function liveIndexPath (\n720\t    repo:string,\n721\t    gitDir:string,\n722\t    env:NodeJS.ProcessEnv\n723\t):string {\n724\t    const live = env.GIT_INDEX_FILE ?? ''\n725\t    if (live === '') return path.join(gitDir, 'index')\n726\t    return path.isAbsolute(live) ? live : path.join(repo, live)\n727\t}\n728\t\n729\t// Copies an index byte for byte to `dst` (mode 0600, truncating). A\n730\t// missing source writes nothing: git treats it as an empty index.\n731\texport async function copyIndex (src:string, dst:string):Promise<void> {\n732\t    let data:Buffer\n733\t    try {\n734\t        data = await fs.readFile(src)\n735\t    } catch (err) {\n736\t        if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === 'ENOENT') return\n737\t        throw wrap(`read ${src}`, err)\n738\t    }\n739\t    let fh:fs.FileHandle\n740\t    try {\n741\t        fh = await fs.open(dst, 'w', 0o600)\n742\t    } catch (err) {\n743\t        throw wrap(`create ${dst}`, err)\n744\t    }\n745\t    try {\n746\t        await fh.writeFile(data)\n747\t    } catch (err) {\n748\t        throw wrap('copy index', err)\n749\t    } finally {\n750\t        await fh.close()\n751\t    }\n752\t}\n753\t\n754\tfunction gitDirOf (repo:string, opts:SnapshotOpts):Promise<string> {\n755\t    return step('resolve git dir', opts,\n756\t        () => run(repo, ['rev-parse', '--absolute-git-dir'], opts))\n757\t}\n758\t\n759\tasync function withTempDir<T> (\n760\t    prefix:string,\n761\t    fn:(dir:string) => Promise<T>\n762\t):Promise<T> {\n763\t    let tmp:string\n764\t    try {\n765\t        tmp = await fs.mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(), prefix))\n766\t    } catch (err) {\n767\t        throw wrap('temp dir', err)\n768\t    }\n769\t    try {\n770\t        return await fn(tmp)\n771\t    } finally {\n772\t        await fs.rm(tmp, { recursive:true, force:true })\n773\t    }\n774\t}\n775\t\n776\t// Pin steps 3-7 inside `tmp`: the worktree written through a private\n777\t// copy of the live index, with denylisted untracked paths excluded.\n778\tasync function writeWorktree (\n779\t    repo:string,\n780\t    live:string,\n781\t    tmp:string,\n782\t    opts:PinOptions\n783\t):Promise<Pin> {\n784\t    const idx = path.join(tmp, 'worktree')\n785\t    await copyIndex(live, idx)\n786\t    const excluded = await denylistedUntracked(repo, {\n787\t        patterns:opts.denylist, signal:opts.signal, env:opts.env,\n788\t    })\n789\t    const env = { GIT_INDEX_FILE:idx }\n790\t    // The positive `.` is required: an exclude-only pathspec matches\n791\t    // nothing. `literal` keeps metacharacters in names from widening.\n792\t    const args = ['add', '-A', '--', '.',\n793\t        ...excluded.map(p => `:(exclude,literal)${p}`)]\n794\t    await step('git add -A (snapshot index)', opts,\n795\t        () => run(repo, args, opts, { env }))\n796\t    const tree = await step('write-tree (worktree)', opts,\n797\t        () => run(repo, ['write-tree'], opts, { env }))\n798\t    return { tree, excluded }\n799\t}\n800\t\n801\t// Writes the working tree, as a review sees it, into one unreferenced\n802\t// tree object. Needs no HEAD and leaves the live index untouched.\n803\texport async function pinWorktree (\n804\t    repo:string,\n805\t    opts:PinOptions = {}\n806\t):Promise<Pin> {\n807\t    const gitDir = await gitDirOf(repo, opts)\n808\t    const live = liveIndexPath(repo, gitDir, opts.env ?? process.env)\n809\t    return withTempDir('roast-pin-',\n810\t        tmp => writeWorktree(repo, live, tmp, opts))\n811\t}\n812\t\n813\t// Records a replayable snapshot: index and worktree trees joined in one\n814\t// composite tree, committed on HEAD (and a differing base), and pointed\n815\t// at by refs/roast/snapshots/<runId>. Throws rather than returning a\n816\t// partial snapshot.\n817\texport async function capture (\n818\t    repo:string,\n819\t    opts:CaptureOptions\n820\t):Promise<Snapshot> {\n821\t    if (opts.runId === '') throw new Error('snapshot: empty run id')\n822\t    if (opts.headOid === '') {\n823\t        throw new Error('repository has no commits; an unborn HEAD ' +\n824\t            'cannot be restored, so no replayable snapshot is possible')\n825\t    }\n826\t    const gitDir = await gitDirOf(repo, opts)\n827\t    const live = liveIndexPath(repo, gitDir, opts.env ?? process.env)\n828\t    return withTempDir('roast-snapshot-', async tmp => {\n829\t        let indexTree = opts.indexTree ?? ''\n830\t        if (indexTree === '') {\n831\t            const idx = path.join(tmp, 'index')\n832\t            await copyIndex(live, idx)\n833\t            indexTree = await step('write-tree (index)', opts,\n834\t                () => run(repo, ['write-tree'], opts,\n835\t                    { env:{ GIT_INDEX_FILE:idx } }))\n836\t        }\n837\t        const worktree = opts.worktree?.tree ?\n838\t            opts.worktree :\n839\t            await writeWorktree(repo, live, tmp, opts)\n840\t        const composite = await step('mktree', opts, () => run(repo,\n841\t            ['mktree'], opts, {\n842\t                input:`040000 tree ${indexTree}\\tindex\\n` +\n843\t                    `040000 tree ${worktree.tree}\\tworktree\\n`,\n844\t            }))\n845\t        const parents = ['-p', opts.headOid]\n846\t        if (opts.baseOid !== '' && opts.baseOid !== opts.headOid) {\n847\t            parents.push('-p', opts.baseOid)\n848\t        }\n849\t        const commit = await step('commit-tree', opts, () => run(repo,\n850\t            ['commit-tree', composite, ...parents,\n851\t                '-m', `roast run ${opts.runId}`],\n852\t            opts, { env:IDENTITY }))\n853\t        const ref = REF_PREFIX + opts.runId\n854\t        await step(`update-ref ${ref}`, opts,\n855\t            () => run(repo, ['update-ref', ref, commit], opts))\n856\t        return {\n857\t            ref,\n858\t            commit,\n859\t            indexTree,\n860\t            worktreeTree:worktree.tree,\n861\t            head:opts.headOid,\n862\t            base:opts.baseOid,\n863\t            excluded:[...worktree.excluded],\n864\t        }\n865\t    })\n866\t}\n867\t\n868\t// Deletes a run's snapshot ref (the review's cleanup before its run\n869\t// record exists).\n870\texport async function deleteSnapshot (\n871\t    repo:string,\n872\t    runId:string,\n873\t    opts:SnapshotOpts = {}\n874\t):Promise<void> {\n875\t    if (runId === '') throw new Error('delete snapshot: empty run id')\n876\t    const ref = REF_PREFIX + runId\n877\t    await step(`delete ${ref}`, opts,\n878\t        () => run(repo, ['update-ref', '-d', ref], opts))\n879\t}\n880\t\n881\tasync function verify (\n882\t    repo:string,\n883\t    rev:string,\n884\t    opts:SnapshotOpts\n885\t):Promise<string> {\n886\t    try {\n887\t        return await run(repo,\n888\t            ['rev-parse', '--verify', '--quiet', '--end-of-options', rev],\n889\t            opts)\n890\t    } catch {\n891\t        opts.signal?.throwIfAborted()\n892\t        return ''\n893\t    }\n894\t}\n895\t\n896\t// The commit `rev` names, '' when it does not resolve.\n897\texport function revOid (\n898\t    repo:string,\n899\t    rev:string,\n900\t    opts:SnapshotOpts = {}\n901\t):Promise<string> {\n902\t    return verify(repo, `${rev}^{commit}`, opts)\n903\t}\n904\t\n905\texport function headOid (\n906\t    repo:string,\n907\t    opts:SnapshotOpts = {}\n908\t):Promise<string> {\n909\t    return revOid(repo, 'HEAD', opts)\n910\t}\n911\t\n912\t// The tree `rev` names, '' when it does not resolve.\n913\texport function treeOid (\n914\t    repo:string,\n915\t    rev:string,\n916\t    opts:SnapshotOpts = {}\n917\t):Promise<string> {\n918\t    return verify(repo, `${rev}^{tree}`, opts)\n919\t}\n920\t// `roast runs` and `roast runs show` (spec 10, 2.14 and 2.15). The\n921\t// snapshot area adds `checkout` and `prune` to runsSubcommands.\n922\timport fs from 'node:fs/promises'\n923\timport path from 'node:path'\n924\timport type { Argv } from 'yargs'\n925\timport { recordDir } from '../record/dir.js'\n926\timport { reportPath } from '../record/meta.js'\n927\timport { compareBytes } from '../gocompat/strings.js'\n928\timport { textBytes } from '../textsafe/textsafe.js'\n929\timport type { CommandArgs, CommandDef, RunContext } from './command.js'\n930\timport { UsageError } from './errors.js'\n931\t\n932\tconst DEFAULT_LIMIT = 20\n933\t\n934\t// Go bufio.Scanner's maximum token size as Roast configures it.\n935\tconst MAX_LINE = 16 * 1024 * 1024\n936\t\n937\tconst INT64_MIN = -(2n ** 63n)\n938\tconst INT64_MAX = 2n ** 63n - 1n\n939\t\n940\t// A decimal Go int flag value.\n941\tfunction parseLimit (raw:unknown):number {\n942\t    if (raw === undefined) return DEFAULT_LIMIT\n943\t    const s = String(raw)\n944\t    if (!/^[+-]?[0-9]+$/.test(s)) {\n945\t        throw new UsageError(\n946\t            `invalid argument ${JSON.stringify(s)} for \"--limit\" flag`)\n947\t    }\n948\t    const n = BigInt(s)\n949\t    if (n < INT64_MIN || n > INT64_MAX) {\n950\t        throw new UsageError(`invalid argument ${JSON.stringify(s)} ` +\n951\t            'for \"--limit\" flag: value out of range')\n952\t    }\n953\t    return Number(n)\n954\t}\n955\t\n956\t// Splits like Go's bufio.Scanner with ScanLines: on \\n, dropping one\n957\t// trailing \\r; a final unterminated line counts. A line of MAX_LINE\n958\t// bytes or more stops the scan, keeping the lines before it.\n959\texport function scanLines (data:Buffer):Buffer[] {\n960\t    const lines:Buffer[] = []\n961\t    let start = 0\n962\t    while (start < data.length) {\n963\t        let end = data.indexOf(0x0a, start)\n964\t        const terminated = end !== -1\n965\t        if (!terminated) end = data.length\n966\t        if (end - start >= MAX_LINE) break\n967\t        let line = data.subarray(start, end)\n968\t        if (line.length > 0 && line[line.length - 1] === 0x0d) {\n969\t            line = line.subarray(0, line.length - 1)\n970\t        }\n971\t        lines.push(line)\n972\t        start = end + 1\n973\t    }\n974\t    return lines\n975\t}\n976\t\n977\t// Go `%-Ns`: right-padded to n code points, never truncated.\n978\tfunction pad (s:string, n:number):string {\n979\t    const len = [...s].length\n980\t    return len >= n ? s : s + ' '.repeat(n - len)\n981\t}\n982\t\n983\ttype Json = Record<string, unknown>\n984\t\n985\tfunction str (o:Json, key:string):string {\n986\t    const v = o[key] ?? ''\n987\t    if (typeof v !== 'string') throw new TypeError(key)\n988\t    return v\n989\t}\n990\t\n991\tfunction int (o:Json, key:string):number {\n992\t    const v = o[key] ?? 0\n993\t    if (!Number.isSafeInteger(v)) throw new TypeError(key)\n994\t    return v as number\n995\t}\n996\t\n997\t// One index line in the `runs` listing format, or null when the line\n998\t// does not decode the way Go's json.Unmarshal would accept.\n999\texport function formatRunLine (line:string):string|null {\n1000\t    try {\n1001\t        const o:unknown = JSON.parse(line)\n1002\t        if (o === null) return formatRunLine('{}')\n1003\t        if (typeof o !== 'object' || Array.isArray(o)) return null\n1004\t        const r = o as Json\n1005\t        const quick = r.quick ?? false\n1006\t        if (typeof quick !== 'boolean') return null\n1007\t        const files = r.files_reviewed ?? []\n1008\t        if (!Array.isArray(files) ||\n1009\t            files.some(f => f !== null && typeof f !== 'string')) {\n1010\t            return null\n1011\t        }\n1012\t        return `${str(r, 'ts')}  ${pad(str(r, 'run_id'), 25)} ` +\n1013\t            `${pad(str(r, 'mode'), 8)} q=${pad(String(quick), 5)} ` +\n1014\t            `${pad(str(r, 'assessment'), 30)} ` +\n1015\t            `findings=${int(r, 'n_findings')} ` +\n1016\t            `refuted=${int(r, 'n_refuted')} files=${files.length}  ` +\n1017\t            `${str(r, 'repo')}\\n`\n1018\t    } catch {\n1019\t        return null\n1020\t    }\n1021\t}\n1022\t\n1023\t// Every index line across the monthly files, oldest first.\n1024\tasync function indexLines (dir:string):Promise<Buffer[]> {\n1025\t    let names:string[]\n1026\t    try {\n1027\t        names = await fs.readdir(dir)\n1028\t    } catch {\n1029\t        return []\n1030\t    }\n1031\t    const files = names\n1032\t        .filter(n => n.startsWith('runs-') && n.endsWith('.jsonl'))\n1033\t        .sort(compareBytes)\n1034\t    const lines:Buffer[] = []\n1035\t    for (const name of files) {\n1036\t        let data:Buffer\n1037\t        try {\n1038\t            data = await fs.readFile(path.join(dir, name))\n1039\t        } catch {\n1040\t            continue\n1041\t        }\n1042\t        lines.push(...scanLines(data))\n1043\t    }\n1044\t    return lines\n1045\t}\n1046\t\n1047\tasync function listHandler (\n1048\t    args:CommandArgs,\n1049\t    ctx:RunContext\n1050\t):Promise<void> {\n1051\t    const limit = parseLimit(args.limit)\n1052\t    const dir = await recordDir(ctx.cwd, {\n1053\t        env:ctx.env as NodeJS.ProcessEnv,\n1054\t        signal:ctx.signal,\n1055\t    })\n1056\t    if (!dir) {\n1057\t        throw new UsageError('cannot resolve record directory: run ' +\n1058\t            'inside a Git repository')\n1059\t    }\n1060\t    let lines = await indexLines(dir)\n1061\t    if (limit > 0 && lines.length > limit) {\n1062\t        lines = lines.slice(lines.length - limit)\n1063\t    }\n1064\t    if (lines.length === 0) {\n1065\t        ctx.stderr.write(`roast: no run records in ${dir}\\n`)\n1066\t        return\n1067\t    }\n1068\t    if (args.json) {\n1069\t        for (const line of lines) {\n1070\t            ctx.stdout.write(Buffer.concat([line, Buffer.from('\\n')]))\n1071\t        }\n1072\t        return\n1073\t    }\n1074\t    for (const line of lines) {\n1075\t        const out = formatRunLine(line.toString('utf8'))\n1076\t        if (out !== null) ctx.stdout.write(out)\n1077\t    }\n1078\t}\n1079\t\n1080\tasync function showHandler (\n1081\t    args:CommandArgs,\n1082\t    ctx:RunContext\n1083\t):Promise<void> {\n1084\t    const dir = await recordDir(ctx.cwd, {\n1085\t        env:ctx.env as NodeJS.ProcessEnv,\n1086\t        signal:ctx.signal,\n1087\t    })\n1088\t    // Plain errors (exit 4), including invalid IDs such as `..`.\n1089\t    const file = await reportPath(dir, String(args['run-id']))\n1090\t    let content:Buffer\n1091\t    try {\n1092\t        content = await fs.readFile(file)\n1093\t    } catch (err) {\n1094\t        const msg = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)\n1095\t        throw new Error(`run has no report artifact: ${msg}`,\n1096\t            { cause:err })\n1097\t    }\n1098\t    ctx.stdout.write(textBytes(content))\n1099\t}\n1100\t\n1101\tconst showCommand:CommandDef = {\n1102\t    command:'show <run-id>',\n1103\t    describe:'Print the stored report of a run',\n1104\t    handler:showHandler,\n1105\t}\n1106\t\n1107\t// The `runs` subcommands; the snapshot area appends its own.\n1108\texport const runsSubcommands:CommandDef[] = [showCommand]\n1109\t\n1110\texport const runsCommand:CommandDef = {\n1111\t    command:'runs',\n1112\t    subcommands:runsSubcommands,\n1113\t    describe:'List past review runs',\n1114\t    builder:(y:Argv) => y\n1115\t        .option('limit', {\n1116\t            type:'string',\n1117\t            describe:'Show the last N runs (<= 0 for all)',\n1118\t            default:undefined,\n1119\t            defaultDescription:String(DEFAULT_LIMIT),\n1120\t        })\n1121\t        .option('json', {\n1122\t            type:'boolean',\n1123\t            default:false,\n1124\t            describe:'Print raw index lines',\n1125\t        }),\n1126\t    handler:listHandler,\n1127\t}\n1128\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0de572ae-0eae-4979-b0e7-cefd41e0bbfc","uuid":"8a91dcf8-3a29-410e-9070-d11d03a67c26","timestamp":"2026-11-24T02:27:44.011Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-24-runs-checkout-and-prune/0de572ae-0eae-4979-b0e7-cefd41e0bbfc/tool-results/b2gczd9rb.txt","content":"# Area 11: Review snapshots, `runs checkout`, `runs prune`\n\n## Purpose\n\nA review snapshot pins exactly what one review read, as ordinary Git\nobjects in the reviewed repository's own object store, so a run record can\nlater be replayed or inspected against the same bytes. It captures the\nindex (what was staged) and the worktree (tracked files plus untracked,\nnon-ignored files, including uncommitted edits and deletions) as two tree\nobjects, joins them in one composite tree, anchors that tree with a commit,\nand points a private ref at the commit:\n\n```\nrefs/roast/snapshots/<run-id>  ->  commit \"roast run <run-id>\"\n                                     parents: HEAD [, base]\n                                     tree:\n                                       index/     (index tree)\n                                       worktree/  (worktree tree)\n```\n\nThe live index and the user's working tree are never written. Three things\nare deliberately not captured: `.gitignore`d files (`git add -A` skips them,\nmatching Roast's own scope rules), empty directories (Git cannot represent\nthem), and untracked, non-ignored files whose names match the secret\ndenylist (recorded in the run record as `excluded_paths`).\n\nThe same package provides the \"pin\": the worktree half alone, written as an\nunreferenced tree object before inference starts, so every reader in a\nreview (grounding, `read_file`, verifier context) sees one immutable tree\neven when no replayable snapshot can be recorded. The secret denylist is\nalso owned here and consumed by the scope and context areas.\n\nTwo `runs` subcommands operate on snapshots:\n\n- `npx roast runs checkout <run-id> [dir]` materializes a run's snapshot\n  into a detached linked worktree whose `git status`, `git diff HEAD`, and\n  `git diff --cached HEAD` reproduce the original state.\n- `npx roast runs prune` deletes aged refs under `refs/roast/snapshots/`.\n\nRun record layout and `meta.json` as a whole belong to the records area;\nthis file specifies only the `snapshot` block and how it is filled.\n\n## Behavior contract\n\n### Constants\n\n- `REF_PREFIX = \"refs/roast/snapshots/\"`. Prune only ever deletes refs under\n  this prefix.\n- Default ref retention: 30 days (`720h`). Tighter than any artifact\n  retention because the refs live in the user's repository.\n- Snapshot commit identity (environment added to `commit-tree` only):\n  `GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=roast`, `GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=roast@localhost`,\n  `GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=roast`, `GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=roast@localhost`.\n  Capture must not fail on a machine with no `user.email` and must not\n  attribute Roast's object to the user. Dates are not overridden (an\n  inherited `GIT_COMMITTER_DATE` would apply).\n- Commit message: `roast run <run-id>`.\n- `file_modes` string recorded in `meta.json`, verbatim:\n  `regular and executable files plus symlinks; empty directories are not\n  represented` (one line, single space where wrapped here).\n- Temp directory prefixes (under `os.tmpdir()`): `roast-snapshot-`\n  (capture), `roast-pin-` (pin), `roast-checkout-` (checkout default\n  destination).\n\n### Secret denylist\n\nDefault patterns, in this order:\n\n```\n.env*  *.pem  *_rsa  *.key  creds*\nid_rsa*  id_dsa*  id_ecdsa*  id_ed25519*  id_xmss*\ncredentials.json  .npmrc  .pypirc  .netrc  _netrc  .pgpass\n.git-credentials  *.p12  *.pfx\n```\n\n`denylisted(path)`:\n\n1. Lower-case the path and every pattern (case-insensitive file systems\n   open `.ENV` as `.env`).\n2. A path matches if any pattern matches either its basename or the full\n   repo-relative path (`/`-separated), using Go `filepath.Match`\n   semantics: `*` matches any run of characters except `/` (a leading dot\n   is not special), `?` matches one character except `/`, `[...]` is a\n   character class (`^` negates, ranges `a-z`), `\\` escapes the next\n   character. A malformed pattern never matches (the error is swallowed).\n\nSSH keys are matched by key-type prefix so suffixed names\n(`id_ed25519_github`, `id_rsa_work`, `.pub` files) are covered; there is no\nbare `id_*` (it would drop `id_map.go`).\n\n`denylistedUntracked(repo, patterns?)` returns the untracked, non-ignored\npaths matching the denylist, sorted by byte order:\n\n- `patterns` omitted (Go `nil`) means the default list. An explicitly empty\n  list disables filtering and returns `[]` without running Git. No CLI path\n  passes a custom list; it exists for tests.\n- Runs `git -C <repo> ls-files --others --exclude-standard -z`, splits on\n  NUL, drops empty entries, keeps matches.\n- Tracked files are never denylisted here: their content is already in the\n  repository's history.\n\n### Git invocation rules\n\nEvery Git call is `git -C <dir> <args...>` via `execFile`:\n\n- The child inherits the full process environment; per-call extra\n  variables are layered on top (so a per-call `GIT_INDEX_FILE` wins over an\n  inherited one). Calls without extras inherit `GIT_INDEX_FILE` unchanged,\n  except that restore calls clear it (see Restore, deliberate change 12).\n- The result is stdout with leading and trailing whitespace trimmed.\n- On a non-zero exit the error carries the exit status and, when present,\n  trimmed stderr (`<status>: <stderr>`); Git puts every useful message on\n  stderr.\n- Output can be large (`ls-files --others` on a big tree): the runner must\n  not truncate at Node's default `maxBuffer`.\n\n### Live index resolution\n\nThe index a review reads, and therefore the one a snapshot copies:\n\n1. If `GIT_INDEX_FILE` is set in the process environment: that path; a\n   relative value is resolved against the repository toplevel (`repo`).\n   Under the native pre-commit gate Git points it at the temporary index\n   holding exactly the commit's content, which is what `--staged` reviews.\n2. Otherwise `<absolute-git-dir>/index`, where the Git dir comes from\n   `git -C <repo> rev-parse --absolute-git-dir` (per-worktree Git dir for a\n   linked worktree).\n\nCopying the index: copy the file byte for byte to the destination, created\nwith mode `0600` (truncating). A missing source index is not an error and\nnothing is written (Git treats an absent `GIT_INDEX_FILE` as an empty\nindex). Any other read error is `read <src>: <err>`.\n\n### Pin the worktree (`pinWorktree(repo, denylist?)`)\n\nWrites the working tree, as a review sees it, into one tree object. Needs\nno HEAD, writes no ref and no commit, never writes the live index. The\nwritten objects are unreferenced (collectable by `git gc`) until a capture\nanchors them.\n\n1. `git -C <repo> rev-parse --absolute-git-dir`; failure ->\n   `resolve git dir: <err>`.\n2. Make a temp dir `roast-pin-*`; always removed on exit (success or\n   failure).\n3. Copy the live index to `<tmp>/worktree`.\n4. `excluded = denylistedUntracked(repo, denylist)`.\n5. With `GIT_INDEX_FILE=<tmp>/worktree`:\n   `git -C <repo> add -A -- . :(exclude,literal)<p1> :(exclude,literal)<p2>\n   ...` (one exclude per excluded path, in sorted order). The positive `.`\n   pathspec is required (an exclude-only pathspec matches nothing and\n   errors). `literal` stops metacharacters in a filename (for example a\n   file literally named `*.key`) from widening the exclusion. Failure ->\n   `git add -A (snapshot index): <err>`.\n6. With the same `GIT_INDEX_FILE`: `git -C <repo> write-tree`; failure ->\n   `write-tree (worktree): <err>`.\n7. Return `{tree, excluded}`.\n\nBecause `add -A` runs on a private copy, an unmerged (conflicted) live\nindex is resolved in the copy rather than refused, and the live index is\nuntouched.\n\n### Capture (`capture(repo, opts)`)\n\nOptions: `runId` (names the ref), `headOid`, `baseOid`, `denylist?`,\n`indexTree?` (an index tree already pinned by the caller), `worktree?` (a\npin already taken by the caller). Returns the snapshot identity or throws;\nit never returns a partial snapshot.\n\n1. Empty `runId` -> error `snapshot: empty run id`.\n2. Empty `headOid` -> error `repository has no commits; an unborn HEAD\n   cannot be restored, so no replayable snapshot is possible`. (Restore\n   detaches at the original HEAD; without one the only commit-ish is the\n   synthetic composite, a baseline that never existed.)\n3. `git -C <repo> rev-parse --absolute-git-dir`; failure ->\n   `resolve git dir: <err>`.\n4. Make temp dir `roast-snapshot-*`; always removed on exit.\n5. Index tree: if `opts.indexTree` is non-empty use it verbatim. Otherwise\n   copy the live index to `<tmp>/index` and run `git -C <repo> write-tree`\n   with `GIT_INDEX_FILE=<tmp>/index`; failure ->\n   `write-tree (index): <err>`. An unmerged index makes `write-tree`\n   refuse, so capture fails.\n6. Worktree tree: if `opts.worktree.tree` is non-empty use that pin\n   verbatim (tree and excluded list). Otherwise run steps 3-7 of the pin\n   inside this temp dir (copy to `<tmp>/worktree`, denylist, `add -A`,\n   `write-tree`). Reusing caller pins is what guarantees the recorded tree\n   is the reviewed tree.\n7. Composite tree: `git -C <repo> mktree` with stdin exactly\n   `040000 tree <indexTree>\\tindex\\n040000 tree <worktreeTree>\\tworktree\\n`;\n   failure -> `mktree: <err>`.\n8. Commit: `git -C <repo> commit-tree <composite> -p <headOid>\n   [-p <baseOid>] -m \"roast run <runId>\"` with the identity environment.\n   The second parent is added only when `baseOid` is non-empty and differs\n   from `headOid`. Parents keep HEAD and the branch base reachable even\n   after a rebase or branch delete. Failure -> `commit-tree: <err>`.\n9. `git -C <repo> update-ref refs/roast/snapshots/<runId> <commit>` (no\n   old-value check; an existing ref is overwritten). Failure ->\n   `update-ref <ref>: <err>` (for example a read-only `.git`).\n10. Return `{ref, commit, indexTree, worktreeTree, head:headOid,\n    base:baseOid, excluded}`.\n\nThe ref keeps every snapshot object reachable through `git gc --prune=now`.\n\n### Load (`load(repo, runId)`)\n\nThe snapshot is self-describing; nothing else is read.\n\n1. `git -C <repo> rev-parse --verify --quiet <ref>^{commit}`; a failure or\n   empty output -> `no snapshot ref <ref> in <repo> (run predates\n   snapshots, was pruned, or lives in another clone)`.\n2. `rev-parse --verify --quiet <ref>:index`; failure ->\n   `snapshot <ref> has no index/ tree: <err>`.\n3. `rev-parse --verify --quiet <ref>:worktree`; failure ->\n   `snapshot <ref> has no worktree/ tree: <err>`.\n4. `rev-parse --verify --quiet <ref>^1` -> head, `<ref>^2` -> base; each\n   is best-effort (`\"\"` on failure).\n5. `excluded` is not recoverable from the ref and is empty.\n\n### Delete (`deleteSnapshot(repo, runId)`)\n\nEmpty `runId` -> `delete snapshot: empty run id`. Otherwise\n`git -C <repo> update-ref -d refs/roast/snapshots/<runId>`; failure ->\n`delete <ref>: <err>`. Used by the review to avoid leaking an orphan ref\nwhen the run record cannot be initialized.\n\n### Restore (`restore(repo, runId, dir)`)\n\n0. Clear `GIT_INDEX_FILE`: every git call in restore (`worktree add`,\n   `read-tree`, `checkout-index`, and `removeWorktree`) runs with that\n   variable removed from the child environment, so an inherited index\n   (for example inside a hook) never replaces the new worktree's own.\n   Deliberate change 12.\n1. `load(repo, runId)`; its error propagates.\n2. Empty head -> `snapshot <ref> records no HEAD commit; it cannot be\n   restored to a meaningful baseline` (only a hand-made ref can hit this).\n3. `git -C <repo> worktree add --detach --no-checkout <abs> <head>`,\n   where `<abs>` is `dir` resolved against the process working directory\n   once, before any git call (`path.resolve`); every later call in this\n   function uses `<abs>` too. A relative `dir` therefore means the same\n   place for `worktree add`, `read-tree`, and `checkout-index`, from any\n   subdirectory (deliberate change 12);\n   failure -> `git worktree add: <err>`. Git refuses a `dir` that exists\n   and is non-empty; Roast does no check of its own.\n4. In the new worktree (`git -C <abs> ...`):\n   1. `read-tree <worktreeTree>`; failure -> `read-tree (worktree): <err>`.\n   2. `checkout-index -a -f`; failure -> `checkout-index: <err>`. Disk now\n      holds the worktree tree (contents, exec bit, symlinks).\n   3. `read-tree <indexTree>`; failure -> `read-tree (index): <err>`. The\n      index reverts to what was staged while disk is untouched, so paths on\n      disk but absent from the index show as untracked, deleted files show\n      as deleted, and staged versus unstaged edits are distinguished as\n      they were.\n   On any failure in step 4, call `removeWorktree(repo, abs)` (result\n   ignored) before rethrowing, so no half-built worktree is left\n   registered.\n5. Return the loaded identity.\n\nThe restored status equals the original except for denylisted files,\nignored files, and empty directories.\n\nSubmodules (gitlinks) in a snapshot are unsupported and documented as\nsuch: a gitlink entry is not restored as a populated submodule, and no\nbehavior is specified or tested for it.\n\n### Remove worktree (`removeWorktree(repo, dir)`)\n\n`git -C <repo> worktree remove --force <dir>`. If that fails, remove `dir`\nrecursively (errors ignored) and run `git -C <repo> worktree prune`; only\nif the prune also fails, return `worktree remove <dir>: <remove err>`.\n\n### Prune (`prune(repo, olderThanMs, dryRun)`)\n\n1. `olderThan <= 0` -> use the default retention (30 days).\n2. `git -C <repo> for-each-ref\n   --format=%(refname)%09%(committerdate:unix) refs/roast/snapshots/`;\n   failure -> `for-each-ref refs/roast/snapshots/: <err>`.\n3. Process lines in Git's output order (sorted by refname). Skip a line\n   that is blank, has no tab, whose name lacks the prefix, or whose\n   timestamp (trimmed) is not a base-10 integer (for example a ref pointing\n   at a non-commit, whose committer date is empty). Refs of unknown age are\n   never deleted.\n4. `age = now - timestamp`. Skip when `age < olderThan` (a ref exactly at\n   the threshold is pruned).\n5. Otherwise, unless `dryRun`, run `git -C <repo> update-ref -d <ref>`; on\n   failure stop immediately and throw `delete <ref>: <err>` (refs already\n   deleted stay deleted). Each considered ref yields\n   `{ref, runId:<ref minus prefix>, ageMs, deleted}`.\n\nPrune touches only refs. It never removes run records under\n`.roast/runs/` and never runs `git gc`; the unreferenced objects become\ncollectable by Git's own gc.\n\n### Helper lookups\n\n- `headOid(repo)` = `revOid(repo, \"HEAD\")`.\n- `revOid(repo, rev)`: `git -C <repo> rev-parse --verify --quiet\n  <rev>^{commit}`, `\"\"` on failure.\n- `treeOid(repo, rev)`: same with `<rev>^{tree}`, `\"\"` on failure.\n\n### When a review takes snapshots\n\nConfig: `review.snapshot` in `.roast.json`, an optional boolean. Absent or\n`true` means capture; `false` disables capture. There is no environment\nvariable or flag (a `ROAST_SNAPSHOT` variable from older versions has no\neffect). The `init` starter shows it commented out as `\"snapshot\": true`\ninside the `review` block. Schema description: `Record a Git snapshot of\neach review for replay. Default true.`\n\nSequence inside `review`, after trust and config loading:\n\n1. With `--staged`, the context area pins the index tree first\n   (`PinIndexTree`, same live-index rule and copy-then-`write-tree`); its\n   OID is passed to capture as `indexTree`. Without `--staged`,\n   `indexTree` is empty and capture copies the index itself.\n2. Scope is built. An empty scope produces the no-diff report and returns:\n   no run id, no pin, no snapshot.\n3. A run id is minted (records area, `<YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ>-<8 hex>`); the\n   snapshot ref uses the same id.\n4. If the scope is not staged, `pinWorktree(repo)` runs regardless of the\n   setting. On failure the review logs to stderr `roast: could not pin the\n   working tree (<err>); reading it live with .git, ignored, and\n   denylisted paths blocked` and continues with an empty pin; on success\n   the pinned tree becomes the tree every reader uses. A staged scope takes\n   no pin here (capture computes the worktree half itself).\n5. Snapshot capture (`captureSnapshot`), producing the `snapshot` block and\n   caveat strings:\n   - Disabled: `{captured:false, reason:\"disabled by review.snapshot\"}`,\n     caveat `Git snapshot capture was disabled; this run cannot be\n     replayed.` No Git commands, no ref.\n   - `head` = scope's head OID if set, else `headOid(repo)`.\n   - `base` = scope's base OID if set, else `revOid(repo, scope.base)` when\n     the scope has a base rev name, else `\"\"`.\n   - `capture(repo, {runId, headOid:head, baseOid:base, indexTree,\n     worktree:pin})`.\n   - Capture error: `{captured:false, reason:<err message>, head, base}`\n     (head/base omitted when empty), caveat `Git snapshot capture failed:\n     <err message>`. The review continues; capture failure never aborts it.\n   - Success: `diff_pair` is `head..index` for a staged scope, else `head`\n     for a survey (repo) scope, else `head..worktree` when the scope has no\n     base, else `base..worktree`. `base_tree` = `treeOid(repo, source)`\n     where source is `head` for the three `head*` pairs and `base` for\n     `base..worktree`; omitted when the source or the lookup is empty.\n     Caveat `snapshot excluded <N> denylisted untracked path(s)` when the\n     excluded list is non-empty.\n6. If captured, the ref is deleted (`deleteSnapshot`, errors ignored) on\n   every exit path until the run record is initialized (`meta.json` plus\n   the initial artifacts written). Any usage error in between (invalid\n   `--mode`, prompt over the byte ceiling, a failed record write) therefore\n   leaves no orphan ref. After initialization the ref is durable, even if\n   inference later fails or is cancelled.\n7. `meta.json` `scope.base_oid` / `scope.head_oid` fall back to the\n   snapshot block's `base` / `head` when the scope did not pin them.\n8. The caveats go into `meta.json` `caveats`.\n\n### `snapshot` block in `meta.json`\n\nKey order as written by Go; every key except `captured` is omitted when\nempty:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"captured\": true,\n  \"reason\": \"...\",\n  \"ref\": \"refs/roast/snapshots/<run-id>\",\n  \"commit\": \"<oid>\",\n  \"index_tree\": \"<oid>\",\n  \"worktree_tree\": \"<oid>\",\n  \"base_tree\": \"<oid>\",\n  \"head\": \"<oid>\",\n  \"base\": \"<oid>\",\n  \"diff_pair\": \"head..index|head|head..worktree|base..worktree\",\n  \"file_modes\": \"regular and executable files plus symlinks; ...\",\n  \"excluded_paths\": [\"deploy.pem\"]\n}\n```\n\n`reason` appears only when `captured` is false. Snapshot objects are never\ncopied into the run directory or the run bundle; they live in the\nrepository's object store.\n\n### `npx roast runs checkout <run-id> [dir]`\n\n- Arguments: 1 or 2 positionals; any other count is a usage error (exit 2).\n  No flags.\n- Reads the run's `meta.json` through the records area (`readMeta`), which\n  resolves `<run-id>` as a unique prefix of a run directory name under the\n  current Git toplevel's `.roast/runs/`. Any failure (invalid id, outside a\n  repository, no match, ambiguous prefix, missing or unparsable\n  `meta.json`) is a usage error, exit 2, with the records-area message.\n- `snapshot.captured` false -> usage error, exit 2:\n  `run <run-id> has no Git snapshot (<reason>)`, where `<run-id>` is the\n  full id from `meta.json` and `<reason>` may be empty.\n- Destination: `dir` if given, resolved against the working directory\n  once (`path.resolve`) and that absolute path is used by every git call\n  and in the printed output (deliberate change 12); otherwise\n  `<mkdtemp(os.tmpdir()/roast-checkout-)>/<run-id>`. The temp\n  parent is never cleaned up by Roast. A `mkdtemp` failure is a non-usage\n  error (exit 4).\n- `restore(meta.repo, meta.run_id, dest)`, using the repository path\n  recorded in `meta.json`, not the current directory. Failure -> exit 4,\n  message `restore run <run-id>: <err>`.\n- On success: stderr `roast: remove with: git -C <repo> worktree remove\n  --force <dest>` and stdout `<dest>\\n`; exit 0. Stdout carries only the\n  path, so `cd \"$(npx roast runs checkout <id>)\"` works.\n\nErrors print as `roast: <message>` on stderr (or the JSON error shape when\n`--error-format json` applies; CLI area), with exit codes from the shared\nmapping: usage error -> 2, any other error -> 4.\n\n### `npx roast runs prune [--older-than <duration>] [--dry-run]`\n\n- No positionals (any -> usage error, exit 2).\n- `--older-than`: Go duration (`720h`, `1h30m`, `90s`, negative allowed),\n  default `720h0m0s` (30 days). An unparsable value is a flag error, exit\n  2. Zero or negative falls back to 30 days inside prune.\n- `--dry-run`: boolean, default false; lists without deleting.\n- Repository: the toplevel of the current directory\n  (`git rev-parse --show-toplevel`); outside a repository -> usage error\n  `not inside a git repository`, exit 2. Refs are shared by every linked\n  worktree of the clone.\n- Prune error (listing or a delete failing) -> exit 4; nothing is printed\n  to stdout, even for refs deleted before the failure.\n- No refs selected -> stderr `roast: no snapshot refs older than <d>`,\n  where `<d>` is the flag value formatted as a Go duration (the default\n  prints `720h0m0s`; an explicit `0` prints `0s` although 30 days was\n  applied). This is kept as Go parity. Exit 0.\n- Otherwise one stdout line per ref, in for-each-ref order:\n  `deleted <ref> (age <age>)`, or `would delete <ref> (age <age>)` with\n  `--dry-run`. `<age>` is the age rounded to the nearest hour (half away\n  from zero) and formatted as a Go duration, so it is always `<N>h0m0s`, or\n  `0s` when it rounds to zero. Exit 0.\n\nGo duration formatting needed here (shared duration helper): zero is `0s`;\na whole number of hours `N > 0` is `<N>h0m0s`; the general form is\n`[-][<h>h][<m>m]<s>[.<frac>]s` with hours and minutes present once a larger\nunit is.\n\n## TypeScript module design\n\n`src/snapshot/`:\n\n- `denylist.ts`\n  - `export const DEFAULT_DENYLIST:readonly string[]`\n  - `export function goPathMatch(pattern:string, name:string):boolean`\n    (Go `filepath.Match` port; malformed pattern -> `false`)\n  - `export function denylisted(path:string,\n    patterns?:readonly string[]):boolean`\n  - `export async function denylistedUntracked(repo:string,\n    opts?:{patterns?:readonly string[], signal?:AbortSignal})\n    :Promise<string[]>`\n- `snapshot.ts`\n  - `export const REF_PREFIX = 'refs/roast/snapshots/'`\n  - `export const DEFAULT_REF_RETENTION_MS = 30 * 24 * 3600 * 1000`\n  - `export interface Pin { tree:string; excluded:string[] }`\n  - `export interface Snapshot { ref:string; commit:string;\n    indexTree:string; worktreeTree:string; head:string; base:string;\n    excluded:string[] }`\n  - `export interface CaptureOptions { runId:string; headOid:string;\n    baseOid:string; denylist?:readonly string[]; indexTree?:string;\n    worktree?:Pin; signal?:AbortSignal }`\n  - `export function liveIndexPath(repo:string, gitDir:string,\n    env:NodeJS.ProcessEnv):string`\n  - `export async function pinWorktree(repo:string,\n    opts?:{denylist?:readonly string[], signal?:AbortSignal}):Promise<Pin>`\n  - `export async function capture(repo:string,\n    opts:CaptureOptions):Promise<Snapshot>`\n  - `export async function load(repo:string, runId:string,\n    signal?:AbortSignal):Promise<Snapshot>`\n  - `export async function deleteSnapshot(repo:string,\n    runId:string):Promise<void>`\n  - `export async function restore(repo:string, runId:string,\n    dir:string, signal?:AbortSignal):Promise<Snapshot>`\n  - `export async function removeWorktree(repo:string,\n    dir:string):Promise<void>`\n  - `export interface Pruned { ref:string; runId:string; ageMs:number;\n    deleted:boolean }`\n  - `export async function prune(repo:string, olderThanMs:number,\n    dryRun:boolean, now?:() => number):Promise<Pruned[]>`\n  - `export async function headOid(repo:string):Promise<string>`,\n    `revOid(repo, rev)`, `treeOid(repo, rev)`\n- `meta.ts` (pure, no I/O besides the injected lookups)\n  - `export interface SnapshotMeta` matching the JSON block above, with\n    optional fields left `undefined` so `JSON.stringify` omits them; key\n    insertion order follows the JSON block.\n  - `export async function captureSnapshot(input:{enabled:boolean;\n    repo:string; runId:string; scope:ScopeLike; indexTree:string;\n    pin:Pin|null}):Promise<{meta:SnapshotMeta; caveats:string[]}>`\n\n`src/cli/runs.ts` adds `checkout` and `prune` subcommands (the `runs` list\nand `show` belong to the records/CLI areas).\n\nDependencies:\n\n- Shared Git runner (scope area): `git(dir, args, {env?, input?, signal?})`\n  returning trimmed stdout and throwing an error with status plus trimmed\n  stderr; inherits `process.env`, merges extras; unbounded output.\n- Records area: `readMeta(prefix)`, run id, `meta.json` writing.\n- Scope area: `gitRoot(start)` for prune; `Scope` fields `headOid`,\n  `baseOid`, `base`, `staged`, `survey`; scope and context areas import\n  `denylisted` / `denylistedUntracked` from here.\n- Context area: `pinIndexTree` (staged index pin), pinned-tree readers.\n- Config area: `review.snapshot` (absent means `true`).\n- CLI area: usage error type, exit code mapping, Go duration parse/format\n  helpers, `textsafe`-sanitized error printing.\n- Temp directories: `fs.mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(), prefix))`, removed\n  with `fs.rm(dir, {recursive:true, force:true})` in `finally`.\n- Debug tracing: namespace `roast:snapshot` (Git subcommand names and\n  refs only; no file contents).\n\n## Deliberate changes\n\n- Debug tracing via `@substrate-system/debug` under `roast:snapshot`\n  (decision 5).\n- Windows is unsupported (decision 2), so path handling assumes `/`.\n- Relative `runs checkout` directory is resolved against the working\n  directory once, used by every git call (spec.md change 12).\n- `restore` clears an inherited `GIT_INDEX_FILE` (spec.md change 12).\n- Otherwise none: ref names, Git commands, `meta.json` fields, command\n  output, and exit codes match Go.\n\n## Test plan\n\nLower seam (`src/snapshot/` against real temp Git repositories; each test\ncreates its own repo with a fixed author identity). A shared \"messy repo\"\nfixture holds: a clean committed file, a staged modification, an unstaged\nmodification, an unstaged deletion, an untracked file, an untracked file\nwith the exec bit, an untracked symlink, a `.gitignore`d secret, and an\nuntracked `deploy.pem`.\n\n1. Round trip fidelity: capture leaves `git status --porcelain` of the\n   source unchanged; after restore, `git diff --cached HEAD` and\n   `git diff HEAD` are byte-identical to the original, status matches\n   except the denylisted file, untracked content is identical, the exec bit\n   survives, the symlink is still a symlink; `excluded` is `[deploy.pem]`\n   (TestRoundTrip).\n2. Secret hygiene: the worktree tree omits the ignored secret and\n   `deploy.pem`, includes `untracked.go`; `deploy.pem` stays on disk\n   (TestSecretHygiene).\n3. Literal pathspec: an untracked file literally named `*.key` is excluded\n   while `keep.go` is captured (TestDenylistLiteralPathspec).\n4. Denylist matching: listed credential names match, including nested,\n   upper-case, and suffixed SSH key names; `main.go`, `credentials.go`,\n   `npmrc.go`, `README.md`, `id_map.go`, `internal/id_test.go`,\n   `identity.go` do not (TestDenylistCoversCredentialFiles). Pure unit\n   test; also cover `goPathMatch` for `*`, `?`, classes, escapes.\n5. Capture fails (no ref written) on an unmerged index\n   (TestCaptureUnmergedIndexFails).\n6. Capture refuses an unborn HEAD (TestCaptureRefusesUnbornHead).\n7. Parents: first parent is HEAD, second is the base when it differs\n   (TestCaptureRecordsParents).\n8. Snapshot objects survive `git gc --prune=now` (TestSurvivesGC).\n9. Prune: with a fresh snapshot and a ref backdated 60 days (commit-tree\n   with old `GIT_AUTHOR_DATE` / `GIT_COMMITTER_DATE`), a 30-day dry run\n   reports exactly the old ref, not deleted, and it still exists; a real\n   run deletes it and the fresh one still loads (TestPrune).\n10. `GIT_INDEX_FILE` honored: with the live index staging `a.txt` and an\n    alternate index staging only `b.txt`, the captured index tree reflects\n    the alternate (TestCaptureHonorsGitIndexFile).\n11. Pin reuse: a pin and the capture given that pin record the same tree\n    (TestPinWorktreeMatchesCapture).\n12. Pin works without HEAD (TestPinWorktreeWithoutHead) and with an\n    unmerged live index, leaving the live index untouched\n    (TestPinWorktreeUnmergedIndex).\n\nCLI seam (`run(argv, io)` in a temp repo with an approved config and the\nfake inference server):\n\n13. `runs checkout` with 0 or 3 positionals exits 2\n    (TestCommandUsageErrorsExitWithUsageCode).\n14. `runs prune --dry-run` in a repository exits 0\n    (TestRunsLocalSubcommandsStillWork).\n15. After a review, `runs checkout <id> <dest>` exits 0 and materializes\n    the reviewed file in `dest`; clean up with `git worktree remove\n    --force` (TestFinderVerifierPipelineWithMockEndpoint). Also: a\n    relative `dest` run from a subdirectory of the repository succeeds and\n    lands under that subdirectory; with `GIT_INDEX_FILE` set to a bogus\n    path in the environment, restore still succeeds and the new\n    worktree's index is its own.\n16. Default config records `snapshot.captured: true` and a ref under\n    `refs/roast/snapshots/`; `review.snapshot: false` records\n    `captured: false` and writes no refs\n    (TestReviewSnapshotSettingInProjectConfig).\n17. With no HEAD, or with `review.snapshot: false`, the review still reads\n    one pinned tree rather than the live worktree\n    (TestReviewPinsCorpusWithoutSnapshot).\n18. `ROAST_SNAPSHOT=0` in the environment does not disable capture\n    (TestRemovedEnvironmentVariablesHaveNoEffect).\n\nNew tests worth adding (behavior the Go code implements but tests do not\npin): checkout of a run whose snapshot was not captured exits 2; prune\noutput lines on stdout and the empty-result message exit 0; a review that\nfails with a usage error after capture (for example invalid `--mode`)\nleaves no ref.\n\n## Go baseline references\n\nAt commit `cd08f10`:\n\n- `internal/snapshot/snapshot.go`\n- `internal/snapshot/snapshot_test.go`, `internal/snapshot/pin_test.go`\n- `internal/cli/runs.go` (`newRunsCheckoutCommand`,\n  `newRunsPruneCommand`)\n- `internal/cli/cli.go` (review sequence around `snapshot.PinWorktree`,\n  `captureSnapshot`, `snapshotDurable`; `exitCode`)\n- `internal/context/tree.go` (`PinIndexTree`)\n- `internal/record/meta.go` (`SnapshotMeta`, `ReadMeta`, `RunDir`)\n- `internal/config/config.go` (`Review.Snapshot`, `SnapshotEnabled`),\n  `internal/config/starter.go`, `roast.schema.json`\n- `internal/cli/cli_test.go`, `internal/cli/settings_test.go`,\n  `internal/cli/hardening_test.go`\n\n## Resolved\n\nResolved (maintainer decisions, see spec.md):\n\n1. A relative `runs checkout` dir is resolved against the working\n   directory once and used by every git call (spec.md change 12).\n2. `runs checkout` / restore clears an inherited `GIT_INDEX_FILE` (spec.md\n   change 12).\n3. Submodules (gitlinks) in snapshots are documented as unsupported\n   (spec.md Out of Scope).\n4. Prune's `0s` message when `--older-than` is <= 0 stays as Go parity.\n// Review snapshots (spec 11): the worktree pin, the replayable capture\n// under refs/roast/snapshots/, its deletion, and revision lookups. The\n// live index and the working tree are never written.\nimport fs from 'node:fs/promises'\nimport os from 'node:os'\nimport path from 'node:path'\nimport { git } from '../git/git.js'\nimport { denylistedUntracked } from './denylist.js'\n\nexport const REF_PREFIX = 'refs/roast/snapshots/'\nexport const DEFAULT_REF_RETENTION_MS = 30 * 24 * 3600 * 1000\n\n// Added to commit-tree only: capture works without user.email and never\n// attributes Roast's object to the user. Dates are not overridden.\nconst IDENTITY:Record<string, string> = {\n    GIT_AUTHOR_NAME:'roast',\n    GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL:'roast@localhost',\n    GIT_COMMITTER_NAME:'roast',\n    GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL:'roast@localhost',\n}\n\nexport interface Pin {\n    tree:string;\n    excluded:string[];\n}\n\nexport interface Snapshot {\n    ref:string;\n    commit:string;\n    indexTree:string;\n    worktreeTree:string;\n    head:string;\n    base:string;\n    excluded:string[];\n}\n\nexport interface SnapshotOpts {\n    signal?:AbortSignal;\n    // The environment git runs with (and GIT_INDEX_FILE is read from);\n    // process.env when absent.\n    env?:NodeJS.ProcessEnv;\n}\n\nexport interface PinOptions extends SnapshotOpts {\n    // Omitted means the default denylist; empty disables filtering.\n    denylist?:readonly string[];\n}\n\nexport interface CaptureOptions extends PinOptions {\n    runId:string;\n    headOid:string;\n    baseOid:string;\n    // An index tree already pinned by the caller (staged reviews).\n    indexTree?:string;\n    // A worktree pin already taken by the caller.\n    worktree?:Pin|null;\n}\n\nfunction message (err:unknown):string {\n    return err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)\n}\n\nfunction wrap (prefix:string, err:unknown):Error {\n    return new Error(`${prefix}: ${message(err)}`, { cause:err })\n}\n\nasync function run (\n    repo:string,\n    args:readonly string[],\n    opts:SnapshotOpts,\n    extra?:{ env?:Record<string, string>, input?:string }\n):Promise<string> {\n    const out = await git(repo, args, {\n        signal:opts.signal,\n        env:opts.env,\n        extraEnv:extra?.env,\n        input:extra?.input,\n    })\n    return out.toString('utf8').trim()\n}\n\n// Runs a git step, wrapping a failure with `prefix` unless aborted.\nasync function step (\n    prefix:string,\n    opts:SnapshotOpts,\n    fn:() => Promise<string>\n):Promise<string> {\n    try {\n        return await fn()\n    } catch (err) {\n        opts.signal?.throwIfAborted()\n        throw wrap(prefix, err)\n    }\n}\n\n// The index a review reads: GIT_INDEX_FILE from `env` (relative values\n// against the toplevel `repo`), else `<gitDir>/index`.\nexport function liveIndexPath (\n    repo:string,\n    gitDir:string,\n    env:NodeJS.ProcessEnv\n):string {\n    const live = env.GIT_INDEX_FILE ?? ''\n    if (live === '') return path.join(gitDir, 'index')\n    return path.isAbsolute(live) ? live : path.join(repo, live)\n}\n\n// Copies an index byte for byte to `dst` (mode 0600, truncating). A\n// missing source writes nothing: git treats it as an empty index.\nexport async function copyIndex (src:string, dst:string):Promise<void> {\n    let data:Buffer\n    try {\n        data = await fs.readFile(src)\n    } catch (err) {\n        if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === 'ENOENT') return\n        throw wrap(`read ${src}`, err)\n    }\n    let fh:fs.FileHandle\n    try {\n        fh = await fs.open(dst, 'w', 0o600)\n    } catch (err) {\n        throw wrap(`create ${dst}`, err)\n    }\n    try {\n        await fh.writeFile(data)\n    } catch (err) {\n        throw wrap('copy index', err)\n    } finally {\n        await fh.close()\n    }\n}\n\nfunction gitDirOf (repo:string, opts:SnapshotOpts):Promise<string> {\n    return step('resolve git dir', opts,\n        () => run(repo, ['rev-parse', '--absolute-git-dir'], opts))\n}\n\nasync function withTempDir<T> (\n    prefix:string,\n    fn:(dir:string) => Promise<T>\n):Promise<T> {\n    let tmp:string\n    try {\n        tmp = await fs.mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(), prefix))\n    } catch (err) {\n        throw wrap('temp dir', err)\n    }\n    try {\n        return await fn(tmp)\n    } finally {\n        await fs.rm(tmp, { recursive:true, force:true })\n    }\n}\n\n// Pin steps 3-7 inside `tmp`: the worktree written through a private\n// copy of the live index, with denylisted untracked paths excluded.\nasync function writeWorktree (\n    repo:string,\n    live:string,\n    tmp:string,\n    opts:PinOptions\n):Promise<Pin> {\n    const idx = path.join(tmp, 'worktree')\n    await copyIndex(live, idx)\n    const excluded = await denylistedUntracked(repo, {\n        patterns:opts.denylist, signal:opts.signal, env:opts.env,\n    })\n    const env = { GIT_INDEX_FILE:idx }\n    // The positive `.` is required: an exclude-only pathspec matches\n    // nothing. `literal` keeps metacharacters in names from widening.\n    const args = ['add', '-A', '--', '.',\n        ...excluded.map(p => `:(exclude,literal)${p}`)]\n    await step('git add -A (snapshot index)', opts,\n        () => run(repo, args, opts, { env }))\n    const tree = await step('write-tree (worktree)', opts,\n        () => run(repo, ['write-tree'], opts, { env }))\n    return { tree, excluded }\n}\n\n// Writes the working tree, as a review sees it, into one unreferenced\n// tree object. Needs no HEAD and leaves the live index untouched.\nexport async function pinWorktree (\n    repo:string,\n    opts:PinOptions = {}\n):Promise<Pin> {\n    const gitDir = await gitDirOf(repo, opts)\n    const live = liveIndexPath(repo, gitDir, opts.env ?? process.env)\n    return withTempDir('roast-pin-',\n        tmp => writeWorktree(repo, live, tmp, opts))\n}\n\n// Records a replayable snapshot: index and worktree trees joined in one\n// composite tree, committed on HEAD (and a differing base), and pointed\n// at by refs/roast/snapshots/<runId>. Throws rather than returning a\n// partial snapshot.\nexport async function capture (\n    repo:string,\n    opts:CaptureOptions\n):Promise<Snapshot> {\n    if (opts.runId === '') throw new Error('snapshot: empty run id')\n    if (opts.headOid === '') {\n        throw new Error('repository has no commits; an unborn HEAD ' +\n            'cannot be restored, so no replayable snapshot is possible')\n    }\n    const gitDir = await gitDirOf(repo, opts)\n    const live = liveIndexPath(repo, gitDir, opts.env ?? process.env)\n    return withTempDir('roast-snapshot-', async tmp => {\n        let indexTree = opts.indexTree ?? ''\n        if (indexTree === '') {\n            const idx = path.join(tmp, 'index')\n            await copyIndex(live, idx)\n            indexTree = await step('write-tree (index)', opts,\n                () => run(repo, ['write-tree'], opts,\n                    { env:{ GIT_INDEX_FILE:idx } }))\n        }\n        const worktree = opts.worktree?.tree ?\n            opts.worktree :\n            await writeWorktree(repo, live, tmp, opts)\n        const composite = await step('mktree', opts, () => run(repo,\n            ['mktree'], opts, {\n                input:`040000 tree ${indexTree}\\tindex\\n` +\n                    `040000 tree ${worktree.tree}\\tworktree\\n`,\n            }))\n        const parents = ['-p', opts.headOid]\n        if (opts.baseOid !== '' && opts.baseOid !== opts.headOid) {\n            parents.push('-p', opts.baseOid)\n        }\n        const commit = await step('commit-tree', opts, () => run(repo,\n            ['commit-tree', composite, ...parents,\n                '-m', `roast run ${opts.runId}`],\n            opts, { env:IDENTITY }))\n        const ref = REF_PREFIX + opts.runId\n        await step(`update-ref ${ref}`, opts,\n            () => run(repo, ['update-ref', ref, commit], opts))\n        return {\n            ref,\n            commit,\n            indexTree,\n            worktreeTree:worktree.tree,\n            head:opts.headOid,\n            base:opts.baseOid,\n            excluded:[...worktree.excluded],\n        }\n    })\n}\n\n// Deletes a run's snapshot ref (the review's cleanup before its run\n// record exists).\nexport async function deleteSnapshot (\n    repo:string,\n    runId:string,\n    opts:SnapshotOpts = {}\n):Promise<void> {\n    if (runId === '') throw new Error('delete snapshot: empty run id')\n    const ref = REF_PREFIX + runId\n    await step(`delete ${ref}`, opts,\n        () => run(repo, ['update-ref', '-d', ref], opts))\n}\n\nasync function verify (\n    repo:string,\n    rev:string,\n    opts:SnapshotOpts\n):Promise<string> {\n    try {\n        return await run(repo,\n            ['rev-parse', '--verify', '--quiet', '--end-of-options', rev],\n            opts)\n    } catch {\n        opts.signal?.throwIfAborted()\n        return ''\n    }\n}\n\n// The commit `rev` names, '' when it does not resolve.\nexport function revOid (\n    repo:string,\n    rev:string,\n    opts:SnapshotOpts = {}\n):Promise<string> {\n    return verify(repo, `${rev}^{commit}`, opts)\n}\n\nexport function headOid (\n    repo:string,\n    opts:SnapshotOpts = {}\n):Promise<string> {\n    return revOid(repo, 'HEAD', opts)\n}\n\n// The tree `rev` names, '' when it does not resolve.\nexport function treeOid (\n    repo:string,\n    rev:string,\n    opts:SnapshotOpts = {}\n):Promise<string> {\n    return verify(repo, `${rev}^{tree}`, opts)\n}\n// `roast runs` and `roast runs show` (spec 10, 2.14 and 2.15). The\n// snapshot area adds `checkout` and `prune` to runsSubcommands.\nimport fs from 'node:fs/promises'\nimport path from 'node:path'\nimport type { Argv } from 'yargs'\nimport { recordDir } from '../record/dir.js'\nimport { reportPath } from '../record/meta.js'\nimport { compareBytes } from '../gocompat/strings.js'\nimport { textBytes } from '../textsafe/textsafe.js'\nimport type { CommandArgs, CommandDef, RunContext } from './command.js'\nimport { UsageError } from './errors.js'\n\nconst DEFAULT_LIMIT = 20\n\n// Go bufio.Scanner's maximum token size as Roast configures it.\nconst MAX_LINE = 16 * 1024 * 1024\n\nconst INT64_MIN = -(2n ** 63n)\nconst INT64_MAX = 2n ** 63n - 1n\n\n// A decimal Go int flag value.\nfunction parseLimit (raw:unknown):number {\n    if (raw === undefined) return DEFAULT_LIMIT\n    const s = String(raw)\n    if (!/^[+-]?[0-9]+$/.test(s)) {\n        throw new UsageError(\n            `invalid argument ${JSON.stringify(s)} for \"--limit\" flag`)\n    }\n    const n = BigInt(s)\n    if (n < INT64_MIN || n > INT64_MAX) {\n        throw new UsageError(`invalid argument ${JSON.stringify(s)} ` 